WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:30.480
The woman known to history as Queen Mary I of&nbsp;
England was born on the 18th of February 1516&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:39.960
at Greenwich Palace just outside London. 
Mary’s father was King Henry VIII of England, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:47.800
reigning monarch since 1509 and just the second&nbsp;
of the Tudor dynasty which had been established in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:58.360
1485 by Henry’s father, King Henry VII. Her mother&nbsp;
was Queen Catherine of Aragon, the first wife of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:07.240
Henry VIII, and a daughter of King Ferdinand&nbsp;
II of Aragon and Queen Isabella I of Castile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:16.080
the two monarchs whose marriage in 1469 would&nbsp;
eventually bring about the union of Aragon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:24.880
Castile into the Kingdom of Spain. Mary was the&nbsp;
first healthy baby born from Henry and Catherine’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:33.920
marriage, she having suffered multiple stillbirths&nbsp;
and miscarriages since they were wed in 1509.  
&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:42.040
The circumstances of Mary’s birth and the&nbsp;
political events of the years that followed would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:52.080
shape her entire life. Her grandfather, King Henry&nbsp;
VII, had seized the throne of England back in 1485&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:59.400
after defeating King Richard III at the Battle&nbsp;
of Bosworth Field. With hindsight we can say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:06.160
this brought the Wars of the Roses, the series&nbsp;
of civil wars that had wracked England since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:16.320
the 1450s, to an end. During these the Houses of&nbsp;
York and Lancaster had vied for the crown after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:24.560
the mental collapse of King Henry VI from the&nbsp;
early 1450s onwards created a political crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:32.840
Eventually Henry Tudor, a Welsh upstart&nbsp;
with a quite tenuous claim to the throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:43.240
had won out in 1485. Yet several major revolts&nbsp;
occurred in the late 1480s and into the 1490s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:50.360
and there was no guarantee that the Tudor&nbsp;
dynasty would not be overthrown in time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:58.800
just as Henry VI and Richard III had been. This&nbsp;
made the task of producing a male heir that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:05.560
ensure a smooth succession all the greater&nbsp;
in the eyes of Mary’s father, Henry VIII.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.480
While women were not legally disbarred&nbsp;
from succeeding to the throne in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:19.200
it would still be considered very much a&nbsp;
last resort for Mary to become queen one day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:27.960
Her entire early life would be shaped by her&nbsp;
father’s quest to acquire a male heir. 
&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:38.840
When Mary born in 1516, Catherine was only&nbsp;
thirty and Henry was in his mid-twenties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:47.960
The assumption was that a male heir would arrive&nbsp;
eventually. Consequently, during her earlier years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:53.000
the king and his ministers were more&nbsp;
concerned about who Mary would marry one day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.800 --> 00:06:00.240
At the time, England held the balance of power&nbsp;
in Western Europe between Spain and France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.280
who were engaged in a long-running conflict&nbsp;
for hegemony over the Italian peninsula.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:14.600
The logic of English diplomacy was that Mary&nbsp;
would marry either a French or Spanish prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:18.040
to cement an alliance with one side or the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:26.520
She was first betrothed to the Dauphin of France&nbsp;
at just two years old, an engagement which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:34.720
like several others made in the ensuing years,&nbsp;
was abandoned when alliances shifted once again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:43.640
At the age six, Mary was betrothed to her&nbsp;
cousin, the Holy Roman Emperor, Charles V,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:50.440
who was sixteen years her senior, in order&nbsp;
to cement Henry’s alliance with the Emperor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:58.800
against the King of France. Just four years&nbsp;
later, fortunes would shift once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:06.560
when Charles married Isabella of Portugal&nbsp;
instead, an act which enraged Henry.
&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:14.680
Mary would have been blissfully unaware of&nbsp;
these diplomatic negotiations during her youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:22.240
Catherine herself supervised Mary’s early&nbsp;
education, providing foundational religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:28.720
instruction, teaching her daughter to speak&nbsp;
French and Spanish as fluently as English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:35.640
as well as learning Latin, the language&nbsp;
of the Roman Catholic Church and one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:40.400
in which many books were still being&nbsp;
produced during the sixteenth century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:48.080
Mary was also instructed in music and&nbsp;
dance, both of which, like her parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:55.680
she showed a talent for. At four years old, she&nbsp;
honoured a delegation from the King of France with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:03.840
a performance on the virginal, a forerunner of the&nbsp;
harpsichord. Catherine’s own experience of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:11.000
a strong mother like Isabella of Castile, a great&nbsp;
military commander and queen in her own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:17.560
almost certainly influenced her approach&nbsp;
to raising and educating her daughter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:25.360
It is perhaps partly because her mother did&nbsp;
not doubt that Mary could be Queen, that Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:32.960
herself acquired the confidence and conviction&nbsp;
of her right to succeed to the throne one day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:42.240
Mary’s father also adored his daughter, lavishing&nbsp;
frequent gifts on her, and referring to her as “my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:50.560
pearl in the world.” Mary resembled her father in&nbsp;
some respects. She had the same fair complexion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:59.600
pale blue eyes and ruddy cheeks. Like&nbsp;
him, she also loved music and dancing. 
&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:10.000
When Mary was nine years old, in 1525, her&nbsp;
father, still lacking a legitimate son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:18.040
made her the de facto Princess of Wales, vaguely&nbsp;
confirming that she was heir to the throne of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:26.880
England. He sent her to Ludlow Castle in the&nbsp;
Welsh marches to “rule” Wales in his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:34.840
and to complete her education. This practice was&nbsp;
typical for a king-in-waiting, but some historians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:41.320
emphasize the fact that, while Mary was treated&nbsp;
in every other way as the heir to the throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:49.680
she was never officially invested with the title&nbsp;
of Princess of Wales, which no woman in English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:58.120
history had then ever held in her own right.&nbsp;
This lack of formal investiture indicates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:05.760
Henry did not really intend that Mary should&nbsp;
be Queen and still planned to have a son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.320
However, the decision to send her to Wales and&nbsp;
provide her with her own independent household&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:21.560
for the first time indicates that the king&nbsp;
was making contingency plans at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:30.240
should a male heir not arrive. Catherine,&nbsp;
after all, was nearly forty years old by then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.960
and her child-bearing years&nbsp;
would soon be behind her. 
&nbsp;

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:42.920
Henry, a spendthrift monarch who completely&nbsp;
reversed the sound economic footing his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:49.680
had placed the English state on, spent generously&nbsp;
on outfitting Mary’s household at Ludlow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:55.920
with everything needed to advertise her&nbsp;
position as heir to the throne of England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.480
There was a throne in Mary’s presence chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:06.640
where she received ambassadors, dignitaries and&nbsp;
petitioners on her father’s behalf in Wales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:15.360
Cloth-of-gold covered the altar in Mary’s chapel&nbsp;
at Ludlow and a staff of literally dozens served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:23.840
at her court. Yet historians have noted that Henry&nbsp;
spent about the same amount of money annually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:32.360
on the household of his illegitimate son, Henry&nbsp;
Fitzroy, born in 1519 to one of his mistresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:41.280
Elizabeth Blount, whom the king had also made&nbsp;
Duke of Richmond and Somerset. Some, therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:49.040
still consider it an open question as to whether&nbsp;
Henry might have been toying in the 1520s and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:58.080
much of the 1530s with the idea of trying to leave&nbsp;
his throne to Richmond, despite his illegitimacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:05.920
In any event, Richmond died in 1536 of&nbsp;
what may have been tuberculosis. 
&nbsp;

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:15.400
What was perhaps especially strange about the&nbsp;
concerns about Mary succeeding as Queen of England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:23.160
was that King Henry II, King Edward IV and&nbsp;
Mary’s own grandfather, King Henry VII,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:32.800
had all claimed to have inherited a legitimate&nbsp;
right to rule from female forebears. Henry VII&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:40.360
had simultaneously borrowed his legitimacy from&nbsp;
his wife, Elizabeth of York, and his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:48.560
Margaret Beaufort. Most scholars, therefore,&nbsp;
assume that Henry VIII, in the absence of a son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:56.000
intended that his daughter would be Queen, but&nbsp;
that her power would be exercised primarily by her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:06.200
husband, until a male heir of hers came of age, as&nbsp;
precedent dictated. Matters changed though in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:14.160
second half of the 1520s after Anne Boleyn,&nbsp;
the daughter of a leading English diplomat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:22.920
Sir Thomas Boleyn, won the king’s attentions at&nbsp;
court. Increasingly disenchanted with his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:31.360
Henry became infatuated with Anne. For&nbsp;
her part, Boleyn played a long game and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:37.840
refused to let Henry enter her bed for&nbsp;
a very long time, arguing with the king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:44.960
that she would not enter a relationship with him&nbsp;
until he gave serious consideration to obtaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:54.600
a divorce from Catherine and then marrying her. If&nbsp;
he made her Queen of England, Anne assured Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.600 --> 00:14:00.320
she would give him the male heir he so&nbsp;
longed for to secure the Tudor dynasty.  
&nbsp;

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:09.000
So began what would soon become known both&nbsp;
in England and at the courts of Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:16.520
as the ‘The King’s Great Matter’, his quest to&nbsp;
obtain a divorce from Mary’s mother Catherine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:22.800
While the Protestant Reformation was already&nbsp;
underway across Europe and was sundering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:30.560
ties between some kingdoms and states with the&nbsp;
Papacy in Rome, England was still very much within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:39.240
the Roman Catholic fold in the 1520s. If Henry&nbsp;
wanted a divorce he had to provide evidence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:47.920
a clear legal basis for doing so to Pope Clement&nbsp;
VII in Rome. Soon an annulment to the marriage was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:55.440
being sought on the grounds that Henry’s union&nbsp;
with Catherine had broken biblical law, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:05.040
she had briefly been married to his older brother&nbsp;
Arthur, who had died in 1502. Henry and Catherine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:12.840
had received a papal dispensation to be wed, when&nbsp;
he became king, because Catherine had claimed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:20.840
the brief marriage to Prince Arthur had never&nbsp;
been consummated. Now, nearly two decades later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:27.560
Henry claimed he was after having an attack&nbsp;
of conscience and needed to divorce Catherine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:33.840
as he had breached the injunction of the book&nbsp;
of Leviticus in the Old Testament which stated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:41.920
“If any man should take his brother’s wife, it is&nbsp;
an unclean thing….they will be childless.”  
&nbsp;

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:52.640
In 1527, Henry secretly made his first overtures&nbsp;
concerning the divorce to Pope Clement VII.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.480 --> 00:16:01.960
Unfortunately for Henry, Emperor Charles V’s&nbsp;
armies had sacked and occupied Rome right around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:09.880
this time. Charles was Queen Catherine’s nephew&nbsp;
and there was no way a Papal dispensation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:16.040
Henry to divorce Catherine would be forthcoming&nbsp;
from Rome so long as the city was in Spanish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:23.840
hands. In any event, even when the situation&nbsp;
in Rome changed in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:31.600
Clement would not prove to be receptive to Henry’s&nbsp;
overtures for a divorce. Thus, Henry would spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:40.120
the next several years pursuing his ‘Great Matter’&nbsp;
through various legal, academic and theological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:48.840
arguments. Eventually he would take the dramatic&nbsp;
decision to split with Rome altogether and declare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:56.840
himself to be the Supreme Head of the Church in&nbsp;
England. In this way he was able to sanction his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.840 --> 00:17:04.360
own divorce from Catherine in 1533 and marry&nbsp;
Anne Boleyn almost immediately thereafter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:11.840
These moves also opened the floodgates for&nbsp;
Protestant reformers like the new Archbishop of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:19.720
Canterbury, Thomas Cranmer, to begin introducing&nbsp;
wide-ranging religious reforms to England. 
&nbsp;

00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:31.720
All of these initiatives had consequences&nbsp;
for Mary between 1527 and 1533. In 1528,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:39.600
Henry recalled her from Ludlow to the court. Her&nbsp;
recall at this time to London suggests that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:48.640
father mistakenly anticipated that he would soon&nbsp;
acquire his divorce, marry Anne and produce a son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:56.240
in which case it would be better to not have Mary&nbsp;
at Ludlow performing the duties of a Princess of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:02.800
Wales. Yet, although Henry would have known&nbsp;
that his divorce would have the consequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:09.760
of making Mary legally illegitimate, he did&nbsp;
not immediately demote her in any other way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:17.640
Perhaps this was simply for practicality’s sake.&nbsp;
After all, Henry could not be certain that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:25.440
would get his divorce, or that Anne would give&nbsp;
birth to a son once they were married. Hence, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:32.560
as Henry pursued the divorce which would make his&nbsp;
daughter illegitimate, she remained Princess Mary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:39.560
her servants continued to wear her badges,&nbsp;
and Henry continued to support her household&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:47.960
financially. The ensuing years were painful&nbsp;
and humiliating for both Catherine and Mary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:55.720
Mother and daughter were subjected to tales of&nbsp;
Henry’s intimacy with Anne at court and Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.720 --> 00:19:03.200
continued to browbeat Catherine for denying that&nbsp;
her marriage to Arthur had been consummated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.200 --> 00:19:11.080
demanding that she agree to dissolve the marriage.&nbsp;
Catherine refused, asserting that their marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:19.000
was valid and that she was rightly the Queen&nbsp;
of England by God’s will. In retaliation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:27.480
Henry forbade her to see or communicate with&nbsp;
Mary until she submitted to Henry’s will. 
&nbsp;

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:37.280
Cut off from her mother and uncertain of her fate&nbsp;
at her father’s hands, fifteen-year-old Mary’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:44.440
health began to suffer. She experienced irregular&nbsp;
menstrual patterns and bouts of crippling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:52.520
depression. It is unclear what caused these&nbsp;
symptoms. Given her later health issues, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:59.640
we will return to, these were most likely the&nbsp;
first signs of some reproductive health issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:06.600
Alternatively, it may have been the general&nbsp;
stress of the situation she found herself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:13.520
as little more than a child of 12 or&nbsp;
13 from the end of the 1520s onwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:22.320
Compounding this was Henry’s drift towards the&nbsp;
small but growing Protestant camp within England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:29.240
something which both Catherine and Mary,&nbsp;
as devout Roman Catholics, found doubly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:36.480
troubling on top of the divorce proceedings.&nbsp;
Henry himself was a doctrinal Catholic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:43.360
but he was an exceptionally impressionable&nbsp;
monarch and as Protestant reformers like Cranmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:50.920
counselled him on how to obtain his divorce, they&nbsp;
also managed to convince him of the reasonableness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:57.000
of some of the new Protestant ideas that were&nbsp;
arriving to England from the continent.  
&nbsp;

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:09.280
In January 1531, Henry received a communication&nbsp;
from the Pope ordering him to separate from Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:15.800
until Rome had made its final decision on&nbsp;
the proposed annulment of his first marriage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:21.880
In response the king addressed&nbsp;
the English Parliament in February&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:30.000
and made clear his intentions to be recognized as&nbsp;
Supreme Head of the Church of England. Parliament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:37.760
acquiesced in principle, and Henry’s new title&nbsp;
was proclaimed, but it would take a year and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:44.800
to obtain a final submission from England’s&nbsp;
clergy and many bishops and leading clerics were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:54.040
removed from their positions when they refused to&nbsp;
acknowledge the Supremacy. As this played out Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.040 --> 00:22:02.080
had moved into Queen Catherine’s chambers and was&nbsp;
living as the de-facto queen. It cannot be doubted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:10.520
that all of this hurt Mary deeply, both in terms&nbsp;
of her familial situation and her religious views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:19.520
One of the characteristics most often ascribed&nbsp;
to Mary Tudor is a remarkable piety and an overly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:27.720
zealous devotion to Catholicism. However, it was&nbsp;
not until much later in life, that her engagement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:34.480
with religion would increase to the point of&nbsp;
becoming especially noteworthy or extreme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:41.440
It is likely that her long separation from her&nbsp;
mother, and the pervasiveness of Protestant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:48.240
reform in England’s halls of power, made her&nbsp;
cleave more closely to her Catholicism. 
&nbsp;

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:59.360
On the 25th of January 1533, nigh on seven&nbsp;
years after he had first begun pursuing her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:07.000
Henry VIII finally married Anne Boleyn in a&nbsp;
secret early morning ceremony in his private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:16.080
chapel at Whitehall Palace. She was already&nbsp;
pregnant. Events then began to proceed apace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:21.640
as Henry and the newly created&nbsp;
Archbishop of Canterbury, Thomas Cranmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:29.920
acted to consolidate Henry’s control of the&nbsp;
Church, to affirm England’s separation from Rome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:37.720
and to finalize his divorce from Catherine&nbsp;
of Aragon, who was now banished from court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:45.720
and living at Kimbolton Castle in Cambridgeshire,&nbsp;
her health declining steeply at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.480 --> 00:23:55.440
In May of 1533, Cranmer declared the King’s first&nbsp;
marriage null and void and his marriage to Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.440 --> 00:24:03.280
as valid. This supposedly made the&nbsp;
now 17-year-old Mary illegitimate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:10.680
yet Henry did not immediately proceed to&nbsp;
declare her as such. In fact, she retained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:16.960
her title and her father’s financial support&nbsp;
for her household during the ensuing months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:25.160
On the 1st of June, Anne Boleyn was crowned&nbsp;
Queen of England, and on the 7th of September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:34.320
she gave birth to her and Henry’s first child.&nbsp;
Much to the king’s dismay it was a girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:42.960
whom they named Elizabeth. Henry proclaimed,&nbsp;
quote, “by God’s grace, boys will follow.” 
&nbsp;

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:51.160
Several weeks after Elizabeth’s birth,&nbsp;
Henry instructed that Mary’s title of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:58.520
Princess should be revoked and henceforth&nbsp;
she should be referred to as “the Lady Mary.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:05.280
Her servants were told to remove her&nbsp;
royal badges and to wear only the king’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:13.640
Sent to live in her new half-sister’s household,&nbsp;
she was allegedly mistreated there on Anne’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:20.800
orders in the period that ensued, though the&nbsp;
veracity of these claims is still disputed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:29.000
Moreover, some of her difficulties were brought&nbsp;
upon herself by Mary. She refused to acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:35.000
the legitimacy of her father’s new marriage and&nbsp;
part of the treatment of her during these years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:41.960
was clearly designed to pressure her into making&nbsp;
a public statement of support for the new queen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:49.160
In this environment a chilly relationship&nbsp;
developed between king and daughter in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:58.840
mid-1530s. In many ways it was a very familial&nbsp;
affair. Mary, who would be twenty before long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:05.360
was proving to be as hard-headed as her&nbsp;
father. Under these conditions her health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.360 --> 00:26:12.440
worsened further and she also became more&nbsp;
devout, taking comfort from daily prayer.
&nbsp;

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:23.640
The following three years were full of both&nbsp;
religious and dynastic upheaval. In March of 1534,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:29.480
the Act of Succession was introduced in&nbsp;
Parliament, followed later in the year by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:37.720
the institution of the Oath of Succession, the Act&nbsp;
of Supremacy, and the Treason Act. Taken together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:45.480
these measures delegitimized Henry’s marriage&nbsp;
to Catherine, legitimized his marriage to Anne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:52.520
set the seal on Henry’s power as head of the&nbsp;
Church of England, and decreed that denial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.520 --> 00:27:02.560
of any of this was a treasonable offence and&nbsp;
punishable by death. In January 1535, Henry’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:09.200
administration began to accelerate reforms by&nbsp;
dissolving England’s religious houses and taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:16.760
them into crown possession. Henry didn’t agree to&nbsp;
this out of any movement towards Protestant views.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:24.120
He was persuaded in this direction because it&nbsp;
led to an enormous cash windfall for the crown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:33.000
through the fire-sale of over 800 religious houses&nbsp;
and their estates. Some prominent individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:40.680
resisted these revolutionary changes. Among them&nbsp;
was the English Lord Chancellor, Sir Thomas More,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:47.600
who was executed for treason for his refusal&nbsp;
to acknowledge Henry’s split with Rome. Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:55.040
also refused to acknowledge her father’s supremacy&nbsp;
over the church. She would have been encouraged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.040 --> 00:28:02.920
all the same, by the sudden fall of Anne Boleyn.&nbsp;
She suffered two miscarriages in quick succession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:11.120
after the birth of Elizabeth and by November&nbsp;
1535 Henry’s attention had already wandered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:18.520
to Anne’s lady-in-waiting, Jane Seymour.&nbsp;
Anne’s days as queen were numbered. 
&nbsp;

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:30.320
On the 7th of January 1536, Mary’s mother,&nbsp;
Catherine of Aragon died. Some who hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:37.880
Anne Boleyn for usurping Catherine’s place&nbsp;
whispered of poison, but Catherine’s health had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:44.600
in fact, been steadily worsening for several&nbsp;
years and she most likely died of cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:53.720
Mary was forbidden to attend her mother’s funeral.&nbsp;
Catherine’s death was devastating for her. In its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:53.720 --> 00:29:00.440
aftermath she began writing to her cousin, Emperor&nbsp;
Charles V, continuing the case which her mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:08.400
had pressed to him from afar for so many years.&nbsp;
However, things changed rather dramatically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:16.880
Only a few months later, Anne was accused of&nbsp;
adultery and treason, imprisoned in the Tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.880 --> 00:29:25.080
and then executed. With this, the now 20-year-old&nbsp;
Mary’s fortunes had seemingly changed for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:32.640
better and she wrote to her father seeking&nbsp;
to reconcile. Here she asked to be allowed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:39.760
return to court, to serve Jane Seymour, who&nbsp;
Henry had quickly taken as his third queen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:48.040
Mary, though, received a cold reply from&nbsp;
Henry’s by now all-powerful chief minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:56.600
Thomas Cromwell, that the king still demanded that&nbsp;
Mary submit to him regarding the Act of Supremacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:05.520
Mary wrote desperately to Cromwell that she had,&nbsp;
quote, “done the utmost that her conscience would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:13.520
suffice.” Cromwell replied that if such were the&nbsp;
case, then Henry would most likely not allow her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:21.080
to return to court in any fashion. In the end,&nbsp;
convinced that she now had no other choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:28.400
Mary relented and on the surface acknowledged&nbsp;
the religious changes that had been carried out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:35.760
in recent time, despite the manner in which&nbsp;
they clashed with her ardent Catholicism. 
&nbsp;

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:45.200
Mary’s circumstances improved markedly after she&nbsp;
returned to court. Henry was happy to welcome her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:53.520
back and father and daughter gradually reconciled.&nbsp;
Henry gave Mary splendid chambers at court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.040 --> 00:31:00.680
as well as princely accommodations at the country&nbsp;
estates of Hunsdon, Beaulieu, and Hatfield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:07.640
A warm relationship also developed between&nbsp;
Mary and her new stepmother, Jane Seymour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:13.920
who is often credited with helping to&nbsp;
restore Henry’s two daughters to favour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:22.320
The relationship between Mary and Elizabeth would&nbsp;
change too over time. Previously, Mary had not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:27.320
been able to develop much sisterly affection&nbsp;
for the half-sister who had replaced her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:36.200
and the 17-year age gap between them did little&nbsp;
to encourage their closeness. But Elizabeth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:44.320
de-legitimization effectively made the two sisters&nbsp;
equals, both the “natural” daughters of a king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:51.800
equally excluded from the line of succession.&nbsp;
This likely made it easier for Mary to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:58.920
a closer and less complicated relationship with&nbsp;
Elizabeth as the latter grew up in the 1540s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:08.200
All England rejoiced as well on the 12th&nbsp;
of October 1537 when Queen Jane gave birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:16.600
to the long desired male heir, Prince Edward.&nbsp;
Being supplanted by a male heir was much easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:24.000
for Mary to accept, since it was the accepted&nbsp;
convention. The return to something approaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:31.400
a normal family life was seen in her standing&nbsp;
as godmother to Prince Edward. Sadly, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:39.640
Jane Seymour died of puerperal fever, then known&nbsp;
as childbed fever, within two weeks of giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:46.240
birth to her son. Mary served as chief mourner at&nbsp;
her stepmother’s funeral. 
&nbsp;

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:52.400
Henry mourned Jane’s death deeply and would not&nbsp;
marry again for two years. By 1539, negotiations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:59.160
were underway for the king’s marriage to Anne of&nbsp;
Cleves, a German principality in the Rhineland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:07.240
Mary was now 23 years old. Her life was&nbsp;
considerably happier and more stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:14.080
than it had been in years. One thing which had&nbsp;
not changed, though, was her unmarried state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:20.080
As the king’s daughter, she could not be&nbsp;
married to an Englishman below her station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:27.440
and if a foreign prince were to be the solution,&nbsp;
the problem arose of who she should marry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:33.600
in order to cultivate a foreign alliance&nbsp;
for England. When Henry’s marriage to Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:41.320
of Cleves was being negotiated, a simultaneous&nbsp;
offer was made for Mary’s hand by Anne’s cousin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.840 --> 00:35:52.040
Duke Philip of Bavaria. In December of 1539, the&nbsp;
Duke arrived quietly in England. Mary and Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:59.120
met in the gardens at Westminster Abbey where they&nbsp;
got to know each other. Philip soon expressed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:06.640
eagerness to marry, despite their religious&nbsp;
incompatibility, as Philip was a Protestant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:13.000
Mary indicated her willingness to consider&nbsp;
the match if it was what her father wished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:20.440
but the proposal was foiled by the swift&nbsp;
annulment of Henry’s marriage to Anne of Cleves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.160 --> 00:36:29.440
This was contracted on the continent before they&nbsp;
ever met and when Anne finally arrived to England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:37.160
Henry was not happy with his new wife, claiming&nbsp;
the court painter, Hans Holbein, who had been sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:44.120
to Germany to paint a picture of Anne for the king&nbsp;
to view back in England, had flattered her in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:50.640
depiction. Henry immediately sought an annulment&nbsp;
to the marriage bonds that had been exchanged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:56.720
between Anne and Henry’s representatives&nbsp;
in Germany, one which was quickly granted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.440 --> 00:37:05.960
In tandem Henry abandoned plans to wed his&nbsp;
daughter to Duke Philip. Strangely, Anne continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:11.800
to live in England afterwards and she and Mary&nbsp;
developed a close enough relationship. 
&nbsp;

00:37:14.120 --> 00:37:23.160
In July of 1540, before the ink was even dry on&nbsp;
his annulment, Henry married as his fifth wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:31.040
Catherine Howard, a 17-year-old niece of the Duke&nbsp;
of Norfolk, the senior-most noble in England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:36.200
Mary found it difficult to get&nbsp;
along with her fourth step-mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:43.680
Catherine was nine years younger than Mary herself&nbsp;
and their personalities were very different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:54.360
The new queen was high-spirited, vivacious and, to&nbsp;
Mary’s mind, rather immature. Queen Catherine soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.360 --> 00:38:02.520
began to complain to King Henry, that Lady Mary&nbsp;
did not show her enough respect, and Mary absented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:10.080
herself from court more frequently. Mary was also&nbsp;
somewhat aggrieved, like many others at court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:16.800
at the manner in which Thomas Cromwell had been&nbsp;
swiftly removed from power and executed on Henry’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:23.960
orders after the Cleves alliance collapsed and&nbsp;
the Duke of Norfolk, Cromwell’s inveterate enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:31.280
in government, engineered the marriage of his&nbsp;
niece to the king. While Cromwell had been central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.280 --> 00:38:38.080
to organizing Henry’s divorce from Mary’s mother&nbsp;
nearly a decade earlier, Mary and he had developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:43.840
a friendly relationship in the years that&nbsp;
followed and there were even rumours at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:51.280
that Cromwell was scheming to marry Henry’s&nbsp;
daughter. That plan, if it ever really existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:57.800
came to naught, as Cromwell was executed on&nbsp;
the 28th of July 1540. 
&nbsp;

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:07.080
Mary had a great deal more reason to resent her&nbsp;
father the following year when Henry reacted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:15.400
a potential coup with the execution of nearly an&nbsp;
entire family of cousins to the Tudors, the Poles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.160 --> 00:39:23.560
Margaret Pole, the Countess of Salisbury, was&nbsp;
the daughter of the long-dead George Plantagenet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.560 --> 00:39:32.920
Duke of Clarence, a younger brother of King Edward&nbsp;
IV who had ruled England between 1461 and 1483&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:40.120
in the midst of the Wars of the Roses. Margaret’s&nbsp;
family was amongst the few descendants of the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:47.640
House of York that remained after the Wars of&nbsp;
the Roses. Each of her four sons represented a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:56.480
potential rival claimant to the throne. Moreover,&nbsp;
the Poles were fervently Roman Catholic. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.480 --> 00:40:03.120
Protestant reforms had advanced to the point of&nbsp;
Henry’s excommunication from the Church, a faction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:10.400
began to form around one scion of the family,&nbsp;
Reginald Pole, who was then living in Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.960 --> 00:40:18.800
to raise him to the throne. Pole had been made&nbsp;
a Cardinal of the Roman Catholic Church in 1536.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.520 --> 00:40:26.960
In the period that followed, Henry made every&nbsp;
effort to have Cardinal Pole sent back to England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:34.080
When he failed, the king instead ordered&nbsp;
the arrest of his relatives in England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:42.040
Then, in one of Henry’s most disreputable acts,&nbsp;
he ordered the execution of the Cardinal’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:51.640
67-year old mother and two of her sons, as&nbsp;
well as imprisoning her grandson, who died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.640 --> 00:41:00.600
most likely of starvation, within a year. Mary&nbsp;
was deeply grieved by Lady Salisbury’s execution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:07.120
as Margaret had been Mary’s governess for&nbsp;
many years in her childhood. The two women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.120 --> 00:41:14.320
had remained close in subsequent years. In years&nbsp;
to come Mary would seek to bring the Poles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:22.600
or what remained of the family, back into the&nbsp;
fold in England. In the early 1540s when all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:29.000
of this occurred, though, she could do little&nbsp;
but swallow whatever resentments she felt.  
&nbsp;

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:39.320
Henry ordered the execution of his fifth wife,&nbsp;
Catherine Howard, for adultery and treason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:48.320
in February of 1542. Less than a year later,&nbsp;
he married his sixth wife, Catherine Parr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.040 --> 00:41:55.080
Like Jane Seymour before him, Catherine&nbsp;
Parr made efforts to be a loving stepmother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.080 --> 00:42:00.880
to all three of Henry’s children, and&nbsp;
encouraged a close family environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:07.240
While it might have been difficult for Mary&nbsp;
to regard Catherine Parr as a maternal figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.760 --> 00:42:14.000
since Catherine was only three years older&nbsp;
than herself, the two had known one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:21.560
for several years and got along well. Both&nbsp;
women were highly educated. Catherine is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:27.520
often credited with influencing Henry to restore&nbsp;
his daughters fully to the line of succession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:35.720
for which Mary was deeply grateful. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
Henry’s declining health over the next years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:44.120
made Mary realize that soon the man that held this&nbsp;
strange family together would be gone and that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:50.560
differences between herself and her siblings&nbsp;
might be harder to reconcile in future. 
&nbsp;

00:42:53.000 --> 00:43:02.640
Henry VIII died on the 28th of January 1547.&nbsp;
Three days later his nine-year-old son from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:11.440
brief marriage to Jane Seymour was proclaimed&nbsp;
as King Edward VI. He was just nine years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:16.800
and so a regency council would rule on&nbsp;
his behalf until the boy came of age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:24.120
Henry was generous to his children in his&nbsp;
will. He left vast estates and movable property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:29.960
to both of his daughters and explicitly&nbsp;
recognized their places in the succession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:38.840
If Edward died without an heir, Mary would&nbsp;
succeed him first, and lacking any issue herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:47.720
the throne would then pass to Elizabeth. Henry&nbsp;
also left Mary extensive estates in East Anglia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:56.200
including lands in the counties of Norfolk,&nbsp;
Suffolk and Essex. Mary now headed her own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.200 --> 00:44:04.160
independent household, an extremely rare state&nbsp;
for an unmarried woman, even a princess.
&nbsp;

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:13.800
Henry’s children drifted apart from one another&nbsp;
after his death. Mary rarely came to court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:14.320 --> 00:44:20.840
preferring to live quietly on her estates in&nbsp;
East Anglia, rather than witness the advance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:28.080
of a radical new Protestant regime as the Seymour&nbsp;
family and others at court such as the Archbishop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:35.160
of Canterbury, Thomas Cranmer, now began to&nbsp;
implement a more thoroughgoing Reformation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:43.080
Henry had been a Catholic in virtually all&nbsp;
respects. He had simply tolerated the Protestants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:49.800
in his government as they had facilitated the&nbsp;
organization of his divorce back in the 1530s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.560 --> 00:44:58.600
However, the 1540s had seen a Catholic backlash&nbsp;
against the Protestants. Now they were free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:05.600
to respond in turn and Cranmer and others were&nbsp;
determined to make England a Protestant nation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.160 --> 00:45:15.640
once Edward succeeded to the throne. In 1549,&nbsp;
Edward’s government passed the Act of Uniformity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:22.240
which abolished the Latin mass and&nbsp;
introduced the English Book of Common Prayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.840 --> 00:45:29.600
compiled by Cranmer, which prescribed a&nbsp;
new, Protestant church service in English.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:38.480
Catholic processions, pilgrimages, and other&nbsp;
ceremonies were prohibited. The Act also called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:46.320
for the removal of all Catholic-style display,&nbsp;
and in churches and abbeys across England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:55.120
stained glass windows were smashed, and crucifixes&nbsp;
and religious images were torn down and burnt. 
&nbsp;

00:45:57.520 --> 00:46:05.120
From her estates away from court, Mary watched on&nbsp;
with some horror at the new attacks on the Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:13.760
Catholic Church. She herself flouted the new&nbsp;
rules and continued to hear the mass in Latin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:19.200
frequently inviting Catholics to&nbsp;
join her. It was during this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:24.400
that Mary’s religious devotion began&nbsp;
to be remarked upon as extraordinary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.040 --> 00:46:32.680
She reportedly began attending mass as many as&nbsp;
four times daily and spending hours at prayer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.360 --> 00:46:40.640
At court Edward and his advisors soon resolved&nbsp;
to put a stop to his sister’s open practice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.640 --> 00:46:50.240
Catholicism. He invited his two sisters to court&nbsp;
for Christmas in 1550, for a rare family reunion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:58.800
An exceptionally embarrassing episode occurred&nbsp;
during the celebrations, when the 13-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.800 --> 00:47:07.000
Edward publicly chastised and denounced his&nbsp;
34-year-old sister in front of the whole court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:16.680
for her disobedience in refusing to accept the new&nbsp;
religious dispensations. Then, a few weeks later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:23.280
Mary was summoned to appear at court once&nbsp;
again. She arrived with a large retinue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:23.800 --> 00:47:31.720
every last one of her attendants visibly carrying&nbsp;
rosary beads. When Edward once again called upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:40.560
her to submit, she invoked the protection of her&nbsp;
cousin, Emperor Charles V. The Spanish ambassador&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:48.240
then stepped forward and threatened Edward with&nbsp;
diplomatic consequences should Lady Mary not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:56.800
permitted to practice her religion freely. Edward&nbsp;
was enraged, yet he backed down all the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.360 --> 00:48:05.080
grudgingly agreeing to allow Mary to continue to&nbsp;
hear the Latin mass, though only in private.
&nbsp;

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:12.200
Mary had scored a great&nbsp;
victory, not just for herself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.760 --> 00:48:19.480
but for Catholics across England, who were&nbsp;
encouraged and emboldened by her resistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:20.200 --> 00:48:26.920
It is important to remember when assessing these&nbsp;
events and what would happen throughout the 1550s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.920 --> 00:48:33.000
that Roman Catholics were still very much&nbsp;
in the majority in England and Wales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:39.280
The Protestants might have come to power&nbsp;
in London and at court, but they probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:39.280 --> 00:48:48.080
accounted for no more than 10 to 20 percent of&nbsp;
the population during Edward’s reign. Hence Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:54.600
became a symbol of Catholic resistance within the&nbsp;
royal family for many people. The struggle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.600 --> 00:49:01.560
her brother no doubt called up terrible memories&nbsp;
of similar conflicts with her father and Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:08.280
began to be afraid that she might have gone too&nbsp;
far. Fearful that Edward might now seize upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:17.040
any excuse to imprison her, Mary began plotting&nbsp;
in 1551 to flee from England to the continent by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:25.000
enlisting the aid of her cousin, Emperor Charles&nbsp;
V, through the Spanish ambassador in London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:25.680 --> 00:49:33.680
She came very close to doing so late that&nbsp;
summer, though eventually she decided against it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:42.640
and resolved to remain in England. It would prove&nbsp;
before long to have been a very wise decision. 
&nbsp;

00:49:45.080 --> 00:49:49.280
Contrary to the traditional&nbsp;
historical characterizations of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:55.720
King Edward VI was not the weak and sickly&nbsp;
boy he has often been portrayed to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.480 --> 00:50:03.840
He was actually a strong, healthy, and athletic&nbsp;
boy, much like his father had been as a young man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:12.760
No one therefore had any serious expectation that&nbsp;
the boy would not grow up. However, during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:22.440
autumn of 1552, he suffered, in quick succession,&nbsp;
an attack of measles, and then one of smallpox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.120 --> 00:50:30.600
These illnesses were catastrophic to his immune&nbsp;
system, and Edward subsequently became perilously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:37.960
weak and sick. Fearing that he might die and&nbsp;
leave the English throne to his Catholic sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.520 --> 00:50:46.160
Edward agreed to a new plan for the royal&nbsp;
succession. This nullified the last wishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:46.160 --> 00:50:53.760
of his father and formally excluded both of&nbsp;
his sisters from the succession. He observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.760 --> 00:51:00.680
that his sisters were both illegitimate and should&nbsp;
not inherit the crown before stating that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:09.160
he died without issue he should be succeeded by&nbsp;
the 15-year-old Lady Jane Grey, the granddaughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.160 --> 00:51:17.040
of Henry VIII’s youngest sister, Mary. At the&nbsp;
same time Edward’s condition worsened in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:24.840
spring and early summer of 1553. He had begun to&nbsp;
cough up blood and many historians believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:31.480
he was now suffering from tuberculosis, though&nbsp;
it may have been a combination of conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.240 --> 00:51:38.960
Knowing that his time was short, Edward&nbsp;
made plans to summon his sisters to court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.520 --> 00:51:45.520
and sanctioned John Dudley, the Duke of&nbsp;
Northumberland, the real power behind the throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.520 --> 00:51:53.200
who had plotted to make Lady Grey the new heir, to&nbsp;
capture and imprison them both when they arrived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:53.800 --> 00:52:00.560
The goal was to ensure that Grey would&nbsp;
be proclaimed Queen without opposition. 
&nbsp;

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:12.000
Mary received Edward’s summons on the 2nd of&nbsp;
July 1553. She was aware that he had been ill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:19.880
but believed he was recovering. Therefore, when&nbsp;
she received his summons, she was not worried,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:27.160
and departed for London with several retainers&nbsp;
the following day. Mary and her retinue only got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:27.160 --> 00:52:34.840
as far as Hertfordshire when they were intercepted&nbsp;
on the road by a supporter of Mary’s from court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.520 --> 00:52:42.040
He had ridden hard to warn her of the trap&nbsp;
her brother had set for her, and informed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:48.920
that Edward was dying and was planning to place&nbsp;
the Protestant Lady Jane Grey on the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.720 --> 00:52:58.520
Mary immediately turned around and rushed back&nbsp;
to East Anglia. Retreating to Framlingham Castle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:58.520 --> 00:53:04.920
she began to mobilize her many supporters&nbsp;
there. She did not have long to prepare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:12.840
On the 6th of July 1553, 15-year-old&nbsp;
Edward died at Greenwich Palace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:20.360
and Lady Jane Grey was quickly proclaimed as Queen&nbsp;
of England by the Duke of Northumberland. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:29.200
she was never crowned, as within days the national&nbsp;
tide shifted overwhelmingly in Mary’s favour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:36.800
The Privy Council, seeing that Edward’s plan for&nbsp;
the succession was proving to be wildly unpopular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:44.240
transferred their support to Mary and&nbsp;
proclaimed her Queen on the 19th of July.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:51.720
Mary rode into London on a wave of&nbsp;
support, while the unfortunate Jane Grey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:58.240
who was nothing more than a pawn in these&nbsp;
machinations, was placed in the Tower of London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.240 --> 00:54:06.080
and eventually executed in early 1554. The&nbsp;
Duke of Northumberland was placed on trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:13.680
found guilty of treason and executed just&nbsp;
a month after Mary ascended as queen.  
&nbsp;

00:54:16.040 --> 00:54:23.280
Mary’s accession to the throne was greeted with&nbsp;
a sense of both continuity and relief to many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:23.280 --> 00:54:30.440
who had feared the excesses of her brother’s&nbsp;
religious reforms. Protestants on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.440 --> 00:54:38.760
fearing the religious upheaval to come, began to&nbsp;
leave England for exile. Most historians identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:46.000
Mary’s major objectives as queen as being the&nbsp;
restoration of the Catholic faith in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:52.920
something which could be secured in the long&nbsp;
run by her marriage, even in her late thirties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.920 --> 00:55:00.080
and the production of a Catholic heir. These&nbsp;
were certainly cherished goals of hers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:00.080 --> 00:55:08.360
but they were certainly not the only ones. In&nbsp;
her mind, and according to conventional wisdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.360 --> 00:55:14.720
they were foremost because nothing else could&nbsp;
be achieved in any lasting way without them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:15.400 --> 00:55:21.120
Without an heir, she could have no&nbsp;
stability, and therefore could not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:27.240
ensure that any reforms she might enact&nbsp;
would endure for decades to come.  
&nbsp;

00:55:29.480 --> 00:55:36.200
The new Queen needed allies to work closely&nbsp;
with her in her new government and soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:42.760
brought figures like Thomas Howard, Duke&nbsp;
of Norfolk, and Bishop Stephen Gardiner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:48.120
who had been marginalized and persecuted&nbsp;
under Edward into her government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.640 --> 00:55:56.520
Gardiner was made Bishop of Winchester, and Lord&nbsp;
Chancellor, and it was he who placed the crown on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.520 --> 00:56:05.640
Mary’s head at her coronation in Westminster Abbey&nbsp;
on the 1st of October 1553. Other initial measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:12.560
concerned the treasury. Mary came to the throne&nbsp;
when it was in a bleak state financially.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.160 --> 00:56:19.240
Her father’s spendthrift habits in the last&nbsp;
decade of his reign and enormously costly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:27.880
wars with France had brought the country close&nbsp;
to bankruptcy. Currency debasement had followed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:35.200
Edward VI had been forced to continue debasement&nbsp;
of the coinage to maintain his own government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:35.200 --> 00:56:42.160
and by the time of his death English coins&nbsp;
were largely just copper coated in silver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:50.520
Mary decided on a two-pronged strategy to resolve&nbsp;
the fiscal problem, a budgetary retrenchment in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:50.520 --> 00:56:58.360
key departments, and a re-organization and&nbsp;
streamlining of the tax system. It would take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.360 --> 00:57:05.720
years, and Mary would not live to see better&nbsp;
days for the English economy, but she laid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:05.720 --> 00:57:11.880
groundwork for an economic recovery in England&nbsp;
in the second half of the sixteenth century. 
&nbsp;

00:57:14.080 --> 00:57:21.720
Mary was indeed her mother’s daughter and&nbsp;
comprehended a nation’s might in much the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:29.320
as her Spanish royal forebears did. As such, she&nbsp;
was keen to promote the development of navigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:29.320 --> 00:57:37.240
and exploration, particularly in pursuit of a&nbsp;
Northeast Passage, a sea route over the north&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.240 --> 00:57:47.480
of Europe to Asia. To this end, Mary issued a&nbsp;
charter to the Muscovy Company in 1555, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:47.480 --> 00:57:55.720
joint-stock company in English history, under the&nbsp;
governorship of famed explorer Sebastian Cabot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.240 --> 00:58:04.440
giving them a trading monopoly in Russia and&nbsp;
the Baltic region. She patronized cartographers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:12.040
and navigators to produce maps and atlases,&nbsp;
and sponsored naval adventurers to sail south&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:18.800
to establish trade relationships with the&nbsp;
west coast of Africa. The Muscovy Company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:18.800 --> 00:58:25.040
soon established contacts with the Duchy of&nbsp;
Muscovy in Russia and began trading therewith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.600 --> 00:58:32.880
an important early development in England’s&nbsp;
overseas colonial and commercial activity. 
&nbsp;

00:58:35.240 --> 00:58:44.080
As the first real Queen Regnant of England, Mary&nbsp;
was in unfamiliar territory. The best course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.080 --> 00:58:49.800
seemed to be to go with conventional wisdom and&nbsp;
find a husband with whom she could produce an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:59.440
heir. This was problematic in and of itself. Most&nbsp;
Englishmen occupied a station too low to wed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:09.040
Queen and the few eligible English Catholic nobles&nbsp;
proved unsuitable. Mary’s cousin, Charles V,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.600 --> 00:59:16.880
proposed marriage to his only son, Prince&nbsp;
Philip of Spain. A portrait of Prince Philip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:16.880 --> 00:59:23.360
painted by the great Italian painter&nbsp;
Titian, was sent to Mary in late 1553.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:31.520
Mary was said to have instantly fallen in love&nbsp;
with the young man in the portrait. This anecdote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:31.520 --> 00:59:38.760
if true, reveals a part of Mary’s personality&nbsp;
that hitherto she had been forced to keep hidden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.760 --> 00:59:47.440
her ardent desire to finally marry and start&nbsp;
a family after literally decades of turmoil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.440 --> 00:59:54.960
in which the subject of her marrying had&nbsp;
become enormously complicated. Now, finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.960 --> 01:00:03.400
Mary was Queen and could pursue marriage&nbsp;
on her own terms. Thus, at the age of 36,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:03.400 --> 01:00:09.760
she insisted on marrying the handsome Prince&nbsp;
Philip of Spain, who was eleven years younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.760 --> 01:00:18.520
than herself at the time.
Mary’s choice of husband was very unpopular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.160 --> 01:00:25.800
Most English people were deeply suspicious of&nbsp;
foreigners and feared that the ever-expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:31.240
Spanish state, which controlled the Low&nbsp;
Countries and much of Italy in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:31.240 --> 01:00:38.680
and had an empire that spanned huge parts of&nbsp;
the Americas, would absorb England politically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:39.240 --> 01:00:47.040
Indeed, Mary’s determination to wed the Spanish&nbsp;
prince sparked a rebellion in January 1554.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:47.680 --> 01:00:56.840
This originated in Kent and was led by Sir&nbsp;
Thomas Wyatt. During it 3,000 rebels marched&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:56.840 --> 01:01:04.280
towards London with the intention of deposing&nbsp;
Mary and possibly placing Mary’s younger sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.280 --> 01:01:12.680
Elizabeth, on the throne, though Elizabeth&nbsp;
was not directly involved in the revolt. Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:20.480
had fewer loyal supporters in the capital than&nbsp;
elsewhere in England and was forced to act quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:20.480 --> 01:01:28.800
to persuade London to act on her behalf. The&nbsp;
queen stood up before restive crowds at London’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:37.240
Guildhall on the 1st of February 1554 and gave&nbsp;
a rousing speech to galvanize support for her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:37.960 --> 01:01:46.280
Thereafter London’s people acted decisively to bar&nbsp;
the city gates to the rebels. Mary’s subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:46.280 --> 01:01:55.280
response was severe. Wyatt and many of the other&nbsp;
leaders of the revolt were executed. Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:55.280 --> 01:02:01.280
who insisted that she had not been involved in&nbsp;
the plot, was arrested and imprisoned in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:01.280 --> 01:02:11.040
Tower for several months, but she was eventually&nbsp;
released. This whole episode made Mary even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:11.040 --> 01:02:18.760
determined to marry Philip, though a Marriage&nbsp;
Act was issued by parliament in April 1554&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:18.760 --> 01:02:25.080
through which it was stated that while Prince&nbsp;
Philip would bear the title “King of England”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.080 --> 01:02:32.520
he would remain the more junior figure in their&nbsp;
marriage so far as English law went. Moreover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:32.520 --> 01:02:37.960
if Mary were to predecease Philip without&nbsp;
their marriage having resulted in children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.480 --> 01:02:44.880
Philip would have no claim to the throne,&nbsp;
which would instead pass to Elizabeth. 
&nbsp;

01:02:47.200 --> 01:02:54.840
Mary finally met Philip for the first time at&nbsp;
Hampton Court Palace on the 23rd of July 1554.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:55.560 --> 01:03:02.480
She was strongly attracted to him, telling him&nbsp;
that she looked forward eagerly to their marriage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.160 --> 01:03:11.760
One of Philip’s aides confided to a correspondent&nbsp;
that Philip did not find the now 37-year-old Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.760 --> 01:03:19.360
attractive at all, but obeyed his father’s&nbsp;
command to marry the Queen of England for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.360 --> 01:03:27.160
dynastic reasons and for the influence Spain&nbsp;
might now be able to exercise in England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.840 --> 01:03:37.720
The two were married at Winchester Cathedral two&nbsp;
days later, on the 25th of July 1554. The wedding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:37.720 --> 01:03:45.320
emphasized Mary’s commitment to simplicity and&nbsp;
tradition. Rather than the fabulous display&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:45.320 --> 01:03:53.360
typical of royal weddings, Mary’s was rather&nbsp;
simple. Her love of fine fashion was evident, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:53.360 --> 01:04:01.040
the bride in a gown of purple and gold tissue,&nbsp;
but she wore a plain gold wedding band. 
&nbsp;

01:04:03.160 --> 01:04:07.560
This period immediately after her&nbsp;
marriage was a happy one for Mary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.320 --> 01:04:14.040
Little more than a month later, she began&nbsp;
to show signs of pregnancy, experiencing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:14.040 --> 01:04:21.160
morning sickness and gaining weight. In the&nbsp;
midst of this, on the 24th of November 1554,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.680 --> 01:04:31.200
Mary received her cousin, Cardinal Reginald&nbsp;
Pole, at court. After his long exile in Italy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:31.200 --> 01:04:38.680
he had arrived home to England as the official&nbsp;
Papal Envoy, one who would help begin the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:38.680 --> 01:04:47.000
of restoring England to the Roman Church. Cardinal&nbsp;
Pole greeted the Queen by quoting the Ave Maria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:54.040
In the Christian scriptures, the angel Gabriel&nbsp;
salutes the Virgin Mary with this prayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.560 --> 01:05:02.160
when delivering the news that she is pregnant&nbsp;
with the Christ child. Mary later reported that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:08.760
as she listened to Cardinal Pole speak she&nbsp;
could feel her baby stirring in her womb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:16.680
In early modern Europe, even if a woman showed&nbsp;
symptoms of pregnancy, she was not considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.680 --> 01:05:24.920
officially pregnant until she felt a “quickening,”&nbsp;
that is, until she felt the baby move inside her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:25.680 --> 01:05:31.800
England rejoiced at news this had occurred&nbsp;
and Parliament promptly passed an act&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:31.800 --> 01:05:40.160
designating Philip as regent for Mary’s heir&nbsp;
should she die in childbirth and the baby survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:40.840 --> 01:05:48.000
In April of 1555, Mary withdrew from&nbsp;
the court for her “confinement”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:57.000
about six weeks before her anticipated due date,&nbsp;
as was the tradition. Mary’s sister Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:57.000 --> 01:06:04.040
was summoned to court to attend her sister,&nbsp;
along with numerous ladies and midwives. 
&nbsp;

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:14.600
The birthing chamber was an extremely rarefied&nbsp;
atmosphere, a world of women, not to be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:23.760
by public eyes. Still, Mary could not resist&nbsp;
flaunting her joy, and on St. George’s Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.280 --> 01:06:30.880
soon after she entered her confinement, Mary&nbsp;
quite deliberately appeared at a window to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:39.480
watch her husband lead the festivities, standing&nbsp;
in a side profile, so that the crowd could see her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:39.480 --> 01:06:47.400
big belly. England waited with bated breath for&nbsp;
the child who would inherit one of the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:47.400 --> 01:06:55.480
royal legacies in European history – both the&nbsp;
throne of England and the Spanish Habsburg Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:56.160 --> 01:07:03.320
On the 1st of June Mary complained of pain and&nbsp;
cramping and the midwives and court physicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.320 --> 01:07:11.720
predicted the birth would occur within days, but&nbsp;
these came and went and still no baby was born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:12.400 --> 01:07:21.120
The summer stretched on and Mary’s attendants&nbsp;
became more uneasy. As the summer passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:28.880
and still no child was born, more and more people&nbsp;
began to doubt the Queen’s pregnancy and rumours&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:38.960
swirled around London. By late July, Mary’s belly&nbsp;
had begun to recede. There would be no baby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:39.680 --> 01:07:46.240
It had been a “phantom pregnancy,” a very&nbsp;
rare condition in which a woman physically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.240 --> 01:07:54.240
manifests all the symptoms of pregnancy, yet&nbsp;
is not pregnant. It is usually caused by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.240 --> 01:08:02.040
combination of both hormonal and psychological&nbsp;
factors. In this case Mary was most likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:09.840
experiencing serious health issues which caused&nbsp;
symptoms that were then interpreted as pregnancy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:18.080
From there her great desire for a child&nbsp;
convinced her that she was in fact pregnant.  
&nbsp;

01:08:20.480 --> 01:08:28.840
On the 1st of August 1555, Mary quietly&nbsp;
exited her confinement and returned to court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.560 --> 01:08:35.920
Philip was deeply disappointed that no child&nbsp;
had been born and, much to Mary’s grief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.480 --> 01:08:41.400
he departed England soon after to&nbsp;
manage his affairs on the continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.080 --> 01:08:49.720
Abandoned by her husband and humiliated in&nbsp;
the eyes of her people, Mary grew despondent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:49.720 --> 01:08:57.120
lapsing into depression. The possibility cannot&nbsp;
be denied that her despair and precarious mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:57.120 --> 01:09:02.680
state might have influenced her&nbsp;
subsequent decisions and policies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:03.440 --> 01:09:12.400
Mary was heard to blame her misfortunes on the&nbsp;
judgment of God, for her “toleration” and “mercy”&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.400 --> 01:09:20.120
towards the Protestant heretics. It is perhaps&nbsp;
no accident then that the religious persecutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:27.520
under Mary’s government accelerated from the&nbsp;
summer of 1555 onwards, though some of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:27.520 --> 01:09:33.120
can also be attributed to the influence&nbsp;
of Pole and others within the government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:33.800 --> 01:09:42.320
The crackdown on the Protestant reforms of her&nbsp;
brother’s reign intensified in 1555 and 1556.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:43.040 --> 01:09:51.240
The Protestant measures of the 1530s and 1540s&nbsp;
were revoked. Mary retroactively declared her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:51.240 --> 01:10:00.000
parents’ marriage to have been valid after all.&nbsp;
She also revoked the 1549 Act of Uniformity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:07.600
and ordered that the mass be once again celebrated&nbsp;
in Latin and churches redecorated in the Catholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:07.600 --> 01:10:16.200
manner. Monasticism was revived, clerical celibacy&nbsp;
was re-established, and married clerics were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:24.240
removed from their offices and ordered to leave&nbsp;
their wives. Roman Catholicism was restored after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:30.880
what many would have perceived as the temporary&nbsp;
Protestant aberration of Edward’s reign. 
&nbsp;

01:10:33.280 --> 01:10:40.080
This satisfied most English people, but more&nbsp;
conservative Catholics still wanted to abolish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:48.120
the royal supremacy and return to the fold of the&nbsp;
Roman Catholic Church. Mary had not changed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:48.120 --> 01:10:55.960
views on the Papal supremacy. The rapprochement&nbsp;
with Rome was a delicate negotiation, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.960 --> 01:11:03.320
take many months to complete and because she&nbsp;
needed their support, the settlement allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:03.320 --> 01:11:10.600
Mary’s wealthiest and most powerful subjects, who&nbsp;
had benefited from Henry VIII’s dissolution of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:10.600 --> 01:11:19.320
monasteries, to keep the land and the abbeys that&nbsp;
had been taken from the Church. Philip insisted on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:28.440
going further and Mary agreed that the old “heresy&nbsp;
laws” should be re-established. Accordingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.440 --> 01:11:38.200
in February 1555, as Mary eagerly anticipated her&nbsp;
first child, the first executions of Protestants&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:38.200 --> 01:11:45.440
by burning at the stake, for which Mary would&nbsp;
become known above all else, were carried out. 
&nbsp;

01:11:47.880 --> 01:11:55.080
When the heresy laws were reintroduced, the slow&nbsp;
trickle of Protestant exiles from England to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.080 --> 01:12:01.760
places like Protestant Geneva on the continent,&nbsp;
which had begun when Mary assumed the throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.280 --> 01:12:11.720
swelled. By 1558 over 800 Protestants had&nbsp;
left England and before Mary’s reign was over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.720 --> 01:12:20.400
approximately 280 would be executed in England,&nbsp;
mostly by the hideous method of burning at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:20.400 --> 01:12:27.840
stake, a punishment which was reserved for&nbsp;
heresy at the time. The first executions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:35.440
took place in February of 1555 and were largely&nbsp;
made up of Protestant leaders and clergy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:35.440 --> 01:12:42.200
but following Mary’s false pregnancy and&nbsp;
humiliation later that year they accelerated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.920 --> 01:12:49.960
Executions for heresy had occurred with some&nbsp;
general regularity during her father’s reign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.960 --> 01:12:57.120
but the scale of them in the mid-1550s&nbsp;
was unprecedented. While they shocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:57.120 --> 01:13:03.960
the English Protestant community, it should not&nbsp;
be assumed that Roman Catholics were appalled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.640 --> 01:13:11.160
Most would have turned out to Smithfield&nbsp;
in London to watch the burnings convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:11.160 --> 01:13:18.800
that they were seeing the end of heretics who&nbsp;
deserved their fiery damnation. Mary’s decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:18.800 --> 01:13:25.960
in ordering the burnings were popular at the time,&nbsp;
as were most of her other religious policies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.720 --> 01:13:33.760
Had she lived longer she would almost certainly&nbsp;
have returned England, where the population was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:33.760 --> 01:13:41.360
still largely Catholic, to the Roman Catholic fold&nbsp;
and stopped the spread of Protestantism there. 
&nbsp;

01:13:43.680 --> 01:13:51.080
That she has been remembered instead as “Bloody&nbsp;
Mary” is in large part owing to the propagandists&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:51.080 --> 01:14:00.600
of the subsequent reign of Elizabeth, and above&nbsp;
all one text, John Foxe’s Acts and Monuments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:01.120 --> 01:14:09.080
more popularly known as Foxe’s Book of&nbsp;
Martyrs. Besides the Bible, this book&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:09.080 --> 01:14:16.400
became for a time in the early modern era the&nbsp;
most widely-read book in the English language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:16.920 --> 01:14:23.360
setting the seal on Mary’s historical&nbsp;
reputation as a zealot and a butcher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:24.080 --> 01:14:31.240
In it the persecution of the Protestants killed&nbsp;
during Mary’s reign was described at length.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:31.960 --> 01:14:36.680
The illustrations which accompanied&nbsp;
the text depicted the burnings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.360 --> 01:14:45.360
Probably the most famous Marian martyr documented&nbsp;
by Foxe was the former Archbishop of Canterbury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:45.360 --> 01:14:53.680
Thomas Cranmer. Cranmer had been imprisoned&nbsp;
as soon as Mary ascended the throne. At trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:53.680 --> 01:15:01.240
he had caved in and renounced his Protestantism.&nbsp;
Typically, it was the practice to spare the lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.240 --> 01:15:08.800
of repentant heretics if they recanted their&nbsp;
previous beliefs in public. Mary, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:08.800 --> 01:15:15.800
was feeling vengeful when it came to Cranmer, a&nbsp;
figure who was central to her mother’s divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.800 --> 01:15:25.680
from Henry, and insisted that he would die. The&nbsp;
plan backfired. When Cranmer was paraded out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:35.280
the 21st of March 1556, he found his courage and&nbsp;
abandoned his plans to offer a further recantation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.280 --> 01:15:43.240
in the hope that he would be spared. Instead, in&nbsp;
a speech he disavowed his earlier recantation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:43.760 --> 01:15:51.320
reaffirmed his Protestant faith and denounced&nbsp;
the Pope as the anti-Christ. He was then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.320 --> 01:15:58.960
roughly pulled down from the platform and taken to&nbsp;
the scaffold where he was committed to the flames.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.640 --> 01:16:06.040
Cranmer died what Foxe would later&nbsp;
describe as a magnificent martyr’s death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.800 --> 01:16:14.720
As the flames were ignited before him, the former&nbsp;
archbishop thrust his right hand into the flames&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.720 --> 01:16:21.400
to burn it first for the sin of signing&nbsp;
the recantation of his Protestant faith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:22.160 --> 01:16:31.680
As Foxe recounted in the Book of Martyrs, his&nbsp;
last words were “Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:32.360 --> 01:16:38.640
I see the heavens open and Jesus standing&nbsp;
at the right hand of God.” 
&nbsp;

01:16:40.920 --> 01:16:47.040
Eventually there was some turning of public&nbsp;
opinion against the execution of Cranmer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.040 --> 01:16:55.720
the other Protestant martyrs. This, combined with&nbsp;
ongoing harvest failures, her false pregnancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.720 --> 01:17:05.640
and her husband’s abandonment of her, tarnished&nbsp;
Mary’s image by 1557 and 1558. These issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:05.640 --> 01:17:15.000
were compounded by war. In March of 1557, Philip,&nbsp;
who had succeeded his own father as King of Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:15.520 --> 01:17:23.560
returned to England to convince Mary that England&nbsp;
should provide troops to support the Spanish in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:23.560 --> 01:17:31.760
their most recent war with the French. Initially&nbsp;
things went well, with a major joint Spanish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.760 --> 01:17:40.040
English victory against the French at the Battle&nbsp;
of St. Quentin in northern France in August 1557.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:40.720 --> 01:17:49.440
But, things took a catastrophic turn in January&nbsp;
1558 when the French seized control of Calais,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:56.840
the last English stronghold on the continent, and&nbsp;
the French port from which many English people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:56.840 --> 01:18:03.280
had assumed England would one day begin&nbsp;
to conquer parts of France yet again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:03.280 --> 01:18:10.520
as they had in the glory days of King Edward III&nbsp;
and King Henry V during the Hundred Years’ War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:11.200 --> 01:18:18.920
This loss was the final blow to Mary’s image&nbsp;
as Queen. Furthermore, the fact that the Pope&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:18.920 --> 01:18:24.280
was allied with the King of France against&nbsp;
Philip strained England’s relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:24.280 --> 01:18:31.080
Catholic Rome, impeding Mary’s goals of a&nbsp;
full return to the Roman Catholic fold.
&nbsp;

01:18:33.240 --> 01:18:41.320
Some months after Philip had returned to Spain&nbsp;
in July 1557, Mary believed that she was pregnant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.320 --> 01:18:49.400
once again. As the months wore on though,&nbsp;
Mary’s abdomen initially swelled, but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:49.400 --> 01:18:58.480
her appetite disappeared as she grew thinner and&nbsp;
weaker. Most suspected that she was terribly ill&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:58.480 --> 01:19:06.760
rather than pregnant. By the spring of 1558 many&nbsp;
were already preparing for the succession of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:06.760 --> 01:19:16.080
half-sister Elizabeth. By the autumn, Mary began&nbsp;
preparing some last wishes. She endowed monastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:16.080 --> 01:19:23.080
and educational institutions with bequests and&nbsp;
scholarships and instructed that daily masses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:23.080 --> 01:19:30.560
should be said for her soul and for those of&nbsp;
her parents. She also requested that her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.560 --> 01:19:37.400
Catherine of Aragon, be exhumed from her grave&nbsp;
at Peterborough Cathedral, so that mother and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:37.400 --> 01:19:45.040
daughter might be buried together at Westminster&nbsp;
Abbey. This last request was never carried out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.760 --> 01:19:53.000
Historians believe that it was ovarian or uterine&nbsp;
cancer that claimed Mary’s life on the 17th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.000 --> 01:20:03.360
November 1558 at 42 years of age. She had probably&nbsp;
been living with it for years, as it would explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:11.640
her earlier phantom pregnancy. Mary was buried&nbsp;
in Westminster Abbey in a modest tomb, one which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:11.640 --> 01:20:19.080
would become much more elaborate when Elizabeth,&nbsp;
forty-five years later, was buried next to her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:19.800 --> 01:20:27.640
Inscribed above their resting place, is a&nbsp;
Latin inscription which translated reads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:28.160 --> 01:20:37.600
“Consorts in realm and tomb, we sisters,&nbsp;
Elizabeth and Mary, here lie down to sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.600 --> 01:20:44.320
in hope of the resurrection.” One was&nbsp;
a Catholic, one was a Protestant. 
&nbsp;

01:20:46.720 --> 01:20:54.680
There would be no repeat of the chaos of Lady Jane&nbsp;
Grey’s revolt in 1553 when Mary died in November&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:54.680 --> 01:21:02.880
1558. A smooth succession occurred with Elizabeth,&nbsp;
the last of Henry VIII’s children from any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:02.880 --> 01:21:10.880
his marriages, succeeding her half-sister. The&nbsp;
daughter of Anne Boleyn was a moderate Protestant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:11.400 --> 01:21:18.600
one who liked the ostentation and trappings&nbsp;
of Roman Catholicism, but skewed doctrinally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.600 --> 01:21:26.880
towards Lutheranism. Thus, a moderate form of&nbsp;
Protestantism was re-imposed from 1558 onwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.880 --> 01:21:32.600
and the Protestant exiles on the continent&nbsp;
began to make their way home to England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:33.320 --> 01:21:40.920
Elizabeth’s reign lasted 45 years and it was&nbsp;
this longevity more so than anything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:47.800
which ensured the success of the Protestant&nbsp;
Reformation after decades of shifts back and forth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.800 --> 01:21:58.000
between Catholicism and Protestantism. By the time&nbsp;
Elizabeth died in 1603 a majority of English men&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:58.000 --> 01:22:05.440
and women had been born after 1558 and had&nbsp;
grown up in the Anglican Protestant faith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.160 --> 01:22:13.360
Mary’s efforts to restore England to the Catholic&nbsp;
fold were a failure as a result in the long run&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:13.360 --> 01:22:19.760
and her reputation was systematically&nbsp;
torn apart by Foxe and others&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:19.760 --> 01:22:24.640
through the books they published&nbsp;
from the late 1550s onwards. 
&nbsp;

01:22:27.120 --> 01:22:34.400
Mary Tudor is generally deemed to have been one of&nbsp;
the most controversial of all England’s monarchs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:35.160 --> 01:22:42.080
The legend of “Bloody Mary” is that England&nbsp;
was drifting benignly towards Protestantism&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:42.080 --> 01:22:47.760
in the sixteenth century when she came&nbsp;
to the throne and inflicted a five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:54.360
reign of terror on her subjects in her&nbsp;
quest to re-impose Roman Catholicism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:55.080 --> 01:23:02.800
The reality is that England was still&nbsp;
broadly Roman Catholic in 1553 and Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:02.800 --> 01:23:09.640
simply engaged in a campaign to reverse what&nbsp;
were perceived by many English people at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:09.640 --> 01:23:18.000
as religious aberrations inflicted on England by&nbsp;
her father and in particular her half-brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:18.640 --> 01:23:25.040
It is also not as though she was alone in&nbsp;
doing this. While they occurred over a longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:25.040 --> 01:23:31.920
time frame, over 200 heretics were killed in&nbsp;
England during the reign of her half-sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:32.480 --> 01:23:39.640
while revolts which had religious overtones during&nbsp;
the reigns of both Henry VIII and Edward VI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:40.160 --> 01:23:49.240
such as the Pilgrimage of Grace in the 1530s and&nbsp;
Kett’s Rebellion in Norfolk in 1549, resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:49.240 --> 01:23:57.840
in hundreds of deaths. The Northern Rising of&nbsp;
1569 was a Roman Catholic revolt in the north of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.840 --> 01:24:06.640
England which was suppressed, following which at&nbsp;
least 700 Catholic insurrectionists were executed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:06.640 --> 01:24:14.120
through martial law by Elizabeth’s government.&nbsp;
If Mary was particularly bloody on account of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:14.120 --> 01:24:22.560
religion, so too were her father, half-brother&nbsp;
and half-sister. That Mary has been remembered&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.560 --> 01:24:30.200
in the fashion that she has been shows how&nbsp;
effectively John Foxe did his job in writing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.200 --> 01:24:36.840
Book of Martyrs. Other than this, we might say&nbsp;
of Mary that she was a woman who had a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:43.200
difficult life owing to her father’s actions&nbsp;
in the late 1520s and throughout the 1530s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:43.760 --> 01:24:49.680
continued to experience difficulties during&nbsp;
the reign of her Protestant half-brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:56.600
and responded when she became queen by&nbsp;
doubling down on her own Catholic convictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:57.120 --> 01:25:03.360
initiating a campaign of repression against&nbsp;
the burgeoning Protestant church in England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:04.120 --> 01:25:12.040
Ultimately, she was a typical monarch of Europe&nbsp;
during the brutality that was the Wars of Religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:12.040 --> 01:25:20.080
unleashed by the Protestant Reformation. 
What do you think of Queen Mary I?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:20.800 --> 01:25:26.560
Does she deserve her reputation as “Bloody&nbsp;
Mary”, the vicious burner of heretics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:27.120 --> 01:25:34.080
or is her poor reputation simply a sign of the&nbsp;
success of subsequent Protestant propagandists&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:42.480
like John Foxe who unfairly condemned a&nbsp;
queen who was popular in her own time? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:42.480 --> 01:25:48.760
let us know in the comment section, and in the&nbsp;
meantime, thank you very much for watching!
&nbsp;

01:27:54.160 --> 01:27:59.160
The woman known to history as Queen&nbsp;
Elizabeth I of England and Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:59.880 --> 01:28:07.240
was born on the 7th of September 1533&nbsp;
at Greenwich Palace near London.
&nbsp;

01:28:08.200 --> 01:28:17.880
Her father was King Henry VIII, the second son of&nbsp;
King Henry VII, who had ruled England between 1485&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.880 --> 01:28:26.200
and 1509, having emerged victorious from the&nbsp;
series of civil wars which wracked England&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:26.200 --> 01:28:34.040
in the fifteenth century known as the Wars of&nbsp;
the Roses. Elizabeth’s mother was Anne Boleyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.600 --> 01:28:44.280
the second wife of Henry VIII and briefly the&nbsp;
Queen Consort of England between 1533 and 1536.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:45.080 --> 01:28:50.960
The circumstances of Elizabeth’s birth would&nbsp;
dictate the first quarter of a century of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:50.960 --> 01:28:57.880
her life. Her parents had hoped for a son&nbsp;
to provide Henry VIII with a long-desired&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:57.880 --> 01:29:06.320
legitimate male heir to continue the Tudor line&nbsp;
and prevent any fall back into the civil wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:06.320 --> 01:29:11.960
which had characterized English&nbsp;
politics in the fifteenth century. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.520 --> 01:29:20.520
while Elizabeth’s birth was celebrated, and she&nbsp;
was promptly proclaimed Henry’s heir presumptive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:20.520 --> 01:29:27.000
jumping past her half-sister Mary, born of&nbsp;
Henry’s first marriage to Catherine of Aragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:27.520 --> 01:29:34.520
there was no denying that Henry and Anne&nbsp;
were disappointed not to have had a boy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:35.320 --> 01:29:42.760
This desire for a male heir had characterized&nbsp;
Henry VIII’s reign since the mid-1520s and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:42.760 --> 01:29:52.680
resulted in his long-running quest for a legal&nbsp;
divorce from his first wife between 1527 and 1533.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:53.360 --> 01:30:01.040
Nevertheless, when a daughter arrived in&nbsp;
1533 the king and queen made the best of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:01.040 --> 01:30:08.240
and claimed that a boy would soon&nbsp;
follow. Meanwhile, in December 1533,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:08.760 --> 01:30:14.640
the three-month-old Princess Elizabeth was&nbsp;
established in her own royal household at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:14.640 --> 01:30:22.400
Hatfield, staffed by numerous servants and a&nbsp;
group of guardians handpicked by her mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:23.080 --> 01:30:28.880
Most of Elizabeth’s closest attendants&nbsp;
were extended Boleyn relatives. 
 
&nbsp;

01:30:31.320 --> 01:30:38.880
Though she was only a baby, Elizabeth was a&nbsp;
chess piece in the English politics of the 1530s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:39.640 --> 01:30:46.680
Before long her father’s patience with her mother&nbsp;
began to wear thin, as Anne suffered multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:46.680 --> 01:30:56.480
miscarriages in the mid-1530s and the son she&nbsp;
had promised the king never arrived. In May 1536,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:57.040 --> 01:31:04.760
after a series of outlandish claims, including&nbsp;
the suggestion that Anne had been in an incestuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.760 --> 01:31:14.480
relationship with her own brother, emerged, Anne&nbsp;
was executed for charges of adultery, incest, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:14.480 --> 01:31:21.720
treason. The charges were probably fabricated as&nbsp;
a means for Henry to dispense of his second wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:22.520 --> 01:31:27.360
Not long thereafter the Boleyn marriage was&nbsp;
annulled on the basis that Henry had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:35.440
seduced into it by some form of withcraft.&nbsp;
This rendered Elizabeth illegitimate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.440 --> 01:31:42.400
within two weeks of Anne’s death Henry was&nbsp;
married to his third wife, Jane Seymour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:43.160 --> 01:31:48.440
He soon ceased supporting Elizabeth’s household&nbsp;
in the fashion that he previously had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:49.160 --> 01:31:57.800
Then, in July of 1536, the English parliament&nbsp;
passed the Succession Act, which stated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:57.800 --> 01:32:05.200
Henry’s marriage to Anne Boleyn had been invalid,&nbsp;
officially rendering Elizabeth illegitimate. 
&nbsp;

01:32:07.400 --> 01:32:16.720
Henry had always demonstrated an attachment to&nbsp;
his eldest daughter, Mary, born in 1516 from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:16.720 --> 01:32:22.760
marriage to Catherine of Aragon, and he had been&nbsp;
quick to welcome her back into the fold in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:22.760 --> 01:32:31.560
1530s once she agreed to end her obstinate refusal&nbsp;
to acknowledge Henry’s divorce of her mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:32.480 --> 01:32:39.400
The king did not immediately make such an effort&nbsp;
for his younger daughter and continued to keep her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:39.400 --> 01:32:46.720
at arm’s length in the years following her&nbsp;
mother’s execution. Indeed, Elizabeth’s governess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:47.280 --> 01:32:53.480
Lady Margaret Bryan, was soon writing to&nbsp;
Thomas Cromwell, Henry’s chief minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:54.000 --> 01:33:00.040
begging him to intercede with the King&nbsp;
on the child’s behalf. In her letter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:00.040 --> 01:33:05.520
Lady Bryan wrote that Elizabeth was growing out&nbsp;
of her clothes quickly and there were insufficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:05.520 --> 01:33:13.280
resources to keep her in a manner befitting a&nbsp;
daughter of the king, legitimate or illegitimate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:14.000 --> 01:33:20.880
Such were the financial straits at Hatfield&nbsp;
that two members of Elizabeth’s household&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:20.880 --> 01:33:26.320
were even charged for poaching deer in&nbsp;
the parklands surrounding the house. 
 
&nbsp;

01:33:28.760 --> 01:33:36.600
The birth in October 1537 of Prince&nbsp;
Edward, Henry’s first legitimate male heir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:37.120 --> 01:33:43.840
changed matters, though it resulted in&nbsp;
the death two weeks later of Jane Seymour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.400 --> 01:33:52.040
Henry’s third wife, owing to post-natal&nbsp;
complications. With a male heir now in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:52.560 --> 01:33:59.480
Elizabeth and Mary were re-integrated&nbsp;
back into the Henrician court more fully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:59.480 --> 01:34:06.000
their positions as either legitimate or&nbsp;
illegitimate being somewhat nullified when there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.000 --> 01:34:14.120
was now a male successor to the throne. Moreover,&nbsp;
before her death and while she was pregnant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:14.120 --> 01:34:21.720
Jane had encouraged the king to restore Elizabeth&nbsp;
to a better position. The belated manner in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.720 --> 01:34:29.360
Henry accepted Elizabeth into the royal household&nbsp;
contrasted sharply with her character. Of all his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.360 --> 01:34:37.280
children, Elizabeth would prove to be the most&nbsp;
like her father. She resembled Henry, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.280 --> 01:34:45.600
same light, reddish hair, pale skin, and ruddy&nbsp;
cheeks, though with her mother Anne’s brown eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:46.320 --> 01:34:52.640
She would also prove herself in years to come to&nbsp;
have the same hard-headedness of her father. 
 
&nbsp;

01:34:55.280 --> 01:35:02.760
Elizabeth received a very good education in the&nbsp;
late 1530s and throughout the 1540s and soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:02.760 --> 01:35:10.320
proved to be one of the most brilliantly learned&nbsp;
women in sixteenth-century Europe. She spoke five&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:10.320 --> 01:35:19.600
languages, English, French, Spanish, Italian and&nbsp;
Latin, and could read Greek and Dutch to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:19.600 --> 01:35:27.080
extent. When an Irish aristocrat visiting court&nbsp;
many years later wanted to gain her attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:27.080 --> 01:35:34.720
he presented her with a small dictionary of key&nbsp;
phrases in the Irish language. Her scholarly work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.720 --> 01:35:42.320
involved translating ancient Latin text such&nbsp;
as Boethius’ The Consolation of Philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:42.320 --> 01:35:51.840
and Tacitus’ Annals. As she was being educated in&nbsp;
the 1540s she grew closer to her younger brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:51.840 --> 01:35:58.880
Prince Edward, who was much closer in age&nbsp;
to her than was Mary. Elizabeth carried the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.880 --> 01:36:04.960
chrisom at Edward’s baptism, they shared some&nbsp;
of the same tutors in the years that followed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:05.480 --> 01:36:12.360
and they were both influenced by the same ardently&nbsp;
Protestant reformers at the Henrician court. 
&nbsp;

01:36:14.920 --> 01:36:20.640
During her early years, Elizabeth found&nbsp;
surrogate mothers in her governesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:20.640 --> 01:36:27.640
and in a succession of stepmothers. The&nbsp;
Protestant German princess, Anne of Cleves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.200 --> 01:36:33.840
whom Henry married when Elizabeth was six&nbsp;
years old, took an interest in Henry’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:33.840 --> 01:36:41.600
youngest daughter. Her marriage to Henry lasted&nbsp;
only a few months before he acquired an annulment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.600 --> 01:36:48.200
but Anne and Elizabeth continued to visit and&nbsp;
write to each other following the divorce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:48.920 --> 01:36:55.920
She did not develop a close relationship with her&nbsp;
father’s fifth wife, Catherine Howard, a niece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:55.920 --> 01:37:05.280
the Duke of Norfolk who wed Henry when she was 17&nbsp;
years old in 1540. She was dead not much over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:05.280 --> 01:37:11.320
year and a half later after committing adultery&nbsp;
with a courtier by the name of Thomas Culpeper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:12.040 --> 01:37:18.720
When this latest stepmother was executed,&nbsp;
Elizabeth drew an important lesson from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:19.440 --> 01:37:25.280
The princess, no doubt remembering what had&nbsp;
happened to her mother as well, remarked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:25.280 --> 01:37:33.920
her childhood friend, Robert Dudley, “I shall&nbsp;
never marry.” This lesson would be reinforced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:33.920 --> 01:37:41.760
over and over again as Elizabeth grew up and she&nbsp;
observed that married women were invariably at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.760 --> 01:37:48.520
the mercy of their husbands, surrendering&nbsp;
power and self-determination to them. 
 
&nbsp;

01:37:50.960 --> 01:37:57.880
Henry’s sixth wife, Catherine Parr, whom Henry&nbsp;
married when Elizabeth was nine years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:57.880 --> 01:38:06.040
was also kind and motherly towards her. Catherine,&nbsp;
who authored several books herself, recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:06.040 --> 01:38:11.800
the young princess’ intellectual potential&nbsp;
and encouraged her in her academic pursuits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:12.520 --> 01:38:19.960
Catherine is also usually credited with persuading&nbsp;
Henry to restore both Mary and Elizabeth to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:19.960 --> 01:38:27.160
line of succession to the throne, after their&nbsp;
younger brother Edward. Beyond these stepmothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.720 --> 01:38:33.280
it was the serving members of Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
household, most of whom were extended relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.280 --> 01:38:41.600
of her mother, Anne Boleyn, with whom Elizabeth&nbsp;
developed the closest relationships, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:41.600 --> 01:38:49.760
her beloved governess, Catherine “Cat” Ashley,&nbsp;
who would remain with Elizabeth as lady-in-waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:49.760 --> 01:38:57.600
when she became queen many years later, while&nbsp;
William Cecil, a rising official esteemed for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:57.600 --> 01:39:04.920
intelligence and abilities, served in his younger&nbsp;
years as the surveyor of Elizabeth’s household.
&nbsp;

01:39:07.440 --> 01:39:13.440
Elizabeth was thirteen years old&nbsp;
when her father died in January 1547.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:14.160 --> 01:39:20.160
It must have been a difficult event full of&nbsp;
conflicting emotions for a girl who by then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:20.160 --> 01:39:26.280
was old enough to know that her father had&nbsp;
sanctioned the execution of her mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.040 --> 01:39:34.200
Henry was generous to his daughters in his will,&nbsp;
allowing each of them an annual income of £3,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:35.760 --> 01:39:44.280
With Henry’s death, nine-year-old Edward became&nbsp;
King Edward VI. A regency council was appointed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:44.280 --> 01:39:51.440
govern for him during his childhood years. This&nbsp;
was first headed by his uncle Edward Seymour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:52.000 --> 01:39:59.640
who became Lord Protector of England and the Duke&nbsp;
of Somerset. The Dowager Queen, Catherine Parr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:00.160 --> 01:40:05.200
invited Elizabeth to live with her&nbsp;
and her new husband, Thomas Seymour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:05.760 --> 01:40:11.160
Somerset’s brother, whom Catherine had&nbsp;
married within months of the king’s death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:11.720 --> 01:40:18.880
something which many people disapproved of.&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s experiences here were less than ideal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:19.600 --> 01:40:25.760
Thomas Seymour was an unscrupulous man,&nbsp;
constantly in pursuit of greater power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:26.480 --> 01:40:33.080
Clearly his marriage to Catherine had not fully&nbsp;
satisfied his ambitions and he began to engage in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:33.080 --> 01:40:40.120
highly inappropriate behaviour towards Elizabeth&nbsp;
once she was ensconced in their household.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:40.840 --> 01:40:46.520
He frequently visited her bedchamber in&nbsp;
the early mornings, still in his nightgown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:47.040 --> 01:40:54.480
sitting on her bed uninvited and tickling her.&nbsp;
Elizabeth began to get up earlier in the mornings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:55.000 --> 01:41:01.120
ensuring that her maids attended her as&nbsp;
early as possible to avoid Seymour’s visits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:01.880 --> 01:41:07.840
It must have been enormously confusing&nbsp;
to her when her stepmother, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:07.840 --> 01:41:14.840
confront her husband about his behavior, began&nbsp;
to participate in the peculiar horseplay. 
 
&nbsp;

01:41:17.280 --> 01:41:25.560
This leaves us with many questions and, if true,&nbsp;
it is a damning indictment of both Seymour and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:25.560 --> 01:41:32.360
Parr. Did Catherine believe that her husband’s&nbsp;
behavior towards her stepdaughter was in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:32.360 --> 01:41:39.840
innocent and all in fun? Was she jealous or&nbsp;
insecure and seeking to keep herself involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:39.840 --> 01:41:45.720
in her husband’s activities? Whatever&nbsp;
Catherine’s beliefs and motives were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:45.720 --> 01:41:53.840
she promptly sent Elizabeth away in May 1548,&nbsp;
when she unexpectedly discovered her husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:53.840 --> 01:42:01.480
and fifteen-year-old stepdaughter, quote: “in an&nbsp;
embrace.” To give Catherine the benefit of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:01.480 --> 01:42:08.680
doubt, she may very well have sent Elizabeth away&nbsp;
to protect her, while she was seemingly unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:08.680 --> 01:42:16.240
control her husband’s behaviour. After being sent&nbsp;
away, Elizabeth never saw her stepmother again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:17.040 --> 01:42:22.240
The two exchanged a few letters&nbsp;
after Elizabeth’s departure, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:22.240 --> 01:42:29.360
Catherine Parr died as a result of complications&nbsp;
from childbirth less than four months later.
 
&nbsp;

01:42:31.920 --> 01:42:39.560
Unfortunately, Elizabeth had not heard the&nbsp;
last of Thomas Seymour. With Catherine dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:46.360
he began actively pursuing Elizabeth’s hand&nbsp;
in marriage. It is difficult to determine how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:46.360 --> 01:42:54.680
Elizabeth felt about this. Seymour was around 40&nbsp;
years old at the time, whereas she was fifteen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.520 --> 01:43:02.920
but such age differences were not entirely unusual&nbsp;
in early modern times when it came to organising&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:02.920 --> 01:43:10.080
political marriages. And a marriage between&nbsp;
one of the king’s uncles and his half-sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:10.080 --> 01:43:18.120
would most certainly be a political match&nbsp;
were it to occur. What foiled Seymour’s plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:18.120 --> 01:43:23.920
was that he acted in secret and when details&nbsp;
of what he was conspiring towards became public&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:23.920 --> 01:43:31.520
it had the look of a political conspiracy by&nbsp;
him. When it was revealed, the Privy Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:31.520 --> 01:43:39.080
had him detained and questioned. Elizabeth was&nbsp;
interrogated closely about the Seymour plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:39.080 --> 01:43:47.760
but she adamantly denied any involvement on her&nbsp;
part in any plans or schemes of which King Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:47.760 --> 01:43:54.160
or the Privy Council did not approve,&nbsp;
including any question of marriage. 
&nbsp;

01:43:56.760 --> 01:44:03.760
The Seymour episode eventually blew over.&nbsp;
Elizabeth likely learned a great deal from it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:04.440 --> 01:44:11.640
For the next two years she passed most of her&nbsp;
time at Hatfield, immersed in her studies, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:11.640 --> 01:44:19.920
wrote regularly and dutifully to her brother, King&nbsp;
Edward. She began to visit court more regularly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.920 --> 01:44:28.640
beginning in or around 1551. There she became the&nbsp;
very model of the virtuous princess, dressing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:28.640 --> 01:44:36.440
modesty and little ostentation. Elizabeth was&nbsp;
Protestant in her religious leanings, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:36.440 --> 01:44:43.680
she was much more moderate than her brother the&nbsp;
king. Edward was emerging as a Calvinist monarch&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.680 --> 01:44:52.080
and had overseen the adoption of a radical form of&nbsp;
Protestantism in the late 1540s and early 1550s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:52.760 --> 01:44:58.600
Elizabeth, conversely, favoured some&nbsp;
doctrinal elements of Protestantism&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:58.600 --> 01:45:06.320
but still admired the pomp and ceremony of&nbsp;
Roman Catholicism. As with so much else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.320 --> 01:45:12.960
she was much like her father in that&nbsp;
regard. In the early summer of 1553,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:12.960 --> 01:45:21.240
with Edward profoundly ill, Elizabeth was warned&nbsp;
by William Cecil that the king was dying and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:21.240 --> 01:45:29.560
there was a plot afoot to imprison both herself&nbsp;
and her half-sister Mary and place Lady Jane Grey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:29.560 --> 01:45:37.400
a great-granddaughter of Henry VII, on the throne&nbsp;
as a Protestant stooge controlled by the Duke of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:37.400 --> 01:45:44.560
Northumberland, who had emerged as the foremost&nbsp;
figure in Edward’s government in the early 1550s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:45.280 --> 01:45:51.840
When Edward VI died on the 6th of&nbsp;
July 1553, possibly from tuberculosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:52.400 --> 01:45:59.680
Lady Jane Grey was proclaimed Queen. Athough&nbsp;
Elizabeth mourned her brother’s passing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:59.680 --> 01:46:04.800
Edward’s attempt to defy their father’s will&nbsp;
and remove both Mary and Elizabeth from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:04.800 --> 01:46:12.320
line of succession rankled her. Thus, when&nbsp;
the Privy Council proclaimed Mary as queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:12.320 --> 01:46:19.360
in defiance of Northumberland’s efforts to make&nbsp;
Lady Jane Grey queen, Elizabeth promptly wrote to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:19.360 --> 01:46:27.360
Mary to signal her support for her cause, despite&nbsp;
Mary’s well-known zealous Roman Catholicism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:28.040 --> 01:46:33.720
Elizabeth accompanied the new queen as&nbsp;
she progressed triumphantly into London&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:33.720 --> 01:46:40.800
in August of 1553, the Grey cause&nbsp;
having collapsed almost immediately. 
&nbsp;

01:46:43.400 --> 01:46:50.040
Mary’s reign would prove tumultuous for&nbsp;
Elizabeth. As a Protestant and Mary’s heir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:50.600 --> 01:46:55.960
she was a figurehead for those English people&nbsp;
who would rather see a Protestant on the throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.600 --> 01:47:02.280
Accordingly, even in instances where she did&nbsp;
not encourage it, she was made a figurehead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:02.280 --> 01:47:09.920
opposition to the Marian regime, most dangerously&nbsp;
in the case of the rebellion in early 1554&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:09.920 --> 01:47:17.320
of Thomas Wyatt, an insurrection which Elizabeth&nbsp;
had nothing to do with but nevertheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:17.320 --> 01:47:24.680
led to her being detained in the Tower of London&nbsp;
for some time. Throughout these unsettling years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:24.680 --> 01:47:30.480
Elizabeth could never be certain that her&nbsp;
life was secure and she cleaved ever closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:30.480 --> 01:47:38.240
to the serving members of her household, whom she&nbsp;
considered her family. These included the Ashleys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.800 --> 01:47:45.400
the Sheltons and William Cecil. She also&nbsp;
grew closer during these years to her friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:45.400 --> 01:47:54.800
Robert Dudley, whom she referred to affectionately&nbsp;
as “sweet Robin.” Robert was a son of John Dudley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:54.800 --> 01:48:01.160
Duke of Northumberland, whom Queen Mary had&nbsp;
executed for his part in the plot to raise Lady&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:01.160 --> 01:48:09.520
Jane Grey to the throne. The apparent closeness&nbsp;
between the princess and Dudley was controversial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:10.160 --> 01:48:14.600
He had been married since&nbsp;
1550 to Amy Robsart. 
 
&nbsp;

01:48:17.200 --> 01:48:25.360
In April of 1555, Elizabeth’s house arrest&nbsp;
abruptly ended, when Queen Mary summoned her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:25.360 --> 01:48:32.040
to court. The queen was labouring under the belief&nbsp;
that she was expecting a child and requested that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:32.040 --> 01:48:38.520
Elizabeth join her in her confinement. Elizabeth&nbsp;
dutifully attended her sister in the birthing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:38.520 --> 01:48:47.720
chamber throughout the summer, but Mary’s due date&nbsp;
came and went and no baby was born. It had been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:47.720 --> 01:48:56.160
phantom pregnancy and Elizabeth remained the heir&nbsp;
to the throne. In the aftermath of this incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:56.160 --> 01:49:02.600
Mary invited Elizabeth to remain at court,&nbsp;
giving her chambers befitting her position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:03.120 --> 01:49:09.480
and allowing her more and more freedom as&nbsp;
time went on. It is possible that Mary’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:09.480 --> 01:49:17.120
conduct in this matter indicated some renewal of&nbsp;
sisterly feeling between the two women. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:17.120 --> 01:49:24.200
Mary may also have invited Elizabeth to remain&nbsp;
at court simply to keep a closer eye on her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:24.880 --> 01:49:33.160
Elizabeth remained at Hatfield for the next two&nbsp;
years. During this time, she and Robert Dudley&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:33.160 --> 01:49:41.360
became closer than ever. He visited her often and&nbsp;
they enjoyed many of the same pursuits, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:41.360 --> 01:49:50.000
riding, hunting, dancing, playing at cards,&nbsp;
and witty conversation. Elizabeth and Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.000 --> 01:49:55.320
seemed not to care about the implications of&nbsp;
their relationship when in each other’s company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:55.880 --> 01:50:02.520
and there was little Elizabeth’s advisors such&nbsp;
as William Cecil could say to dissuade her. 
 
&nbsp;

01:50:05.040 --> 01:50:10.960
Elizabeth endured two years of tension and&nbsp;
uncertainty after her return to Hatfield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.600 --> 01:50:21.160
Then matters changed very dramatically. On the&nbsp;
17th of November 1558, her sister Mary died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:21.680 --> 01:50:29.520
most likely of ovarian or uterine cancer, a likely&nbsp;
explanation for some of the symptoms accompanying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:29.520 --> 01:50:37.920
her phantom pregnancies. Lacking any other heir,&nbsp;
Elizabeth, the last of the children of Henry VIII,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:38.440 --> 01:50:45.320
was promptly proclaimed as Queen Elizabeth I&nbsp;
of England. It was not immediately apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:45.320 --> 01:50:50.360
whether Elizabeth intended to rule as&nbsp;
a Protestant or a Catholic monarch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:51.000 --> 01:50:56.640
For her part, Elizabeth was content that this&nbsp;
ambiguity should last as long as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.160 --> 01:51:04.120
until she could consolidate her power and&nbsp;
establish a government. She well understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:04.120 --> 01:51:10.520
that England was still heavily Catholic, as&nbsp;
were many of the nobles and clergy around her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.240 --> 01:51:17.960
Nearly two decades of separation from Rome had&nbsp;
rendered many English ambivalent about Papal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:17.960 --> 01:51:23.360
supremacy, even those who considered&nbsp;
themselves to be loyal Catholics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:24.080 --> 01:51:30.920
The English parliament thus proved willing to&nbsp;
grant Elizabeth the title of “Supreme Governor&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:30.920 --> 01:51:38.080
of the Church of England” without really knowing&nbsp;
what the queen’s wider religious stance was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.840 --> 01:51:46.880
The Elizabethan religious settlement would only&nbsp;
unfold in the course of 1559 and 1560. 
 
&nbsp;

01:51:49.520 --> 01:51:54.760
Elizabeth understood that she had to proceed&nbsp;
carefully while her reign was still new and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:54.760 --> 01:52:01.000
uncertain. Queen Mary’s body lay in state&nbsp;
at St. James’ Palace until mid-December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:01.520 --> 01:52:06.440
with regular Catholic vigils performed&nbsp;
until her interrment at Westminster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:07.160 --> 01:52:14.480
Elizabeth continued to have the Latin mass&nbsp;
celebrated in her private chapel, but demonstrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:14.480 --> 01:52:22.000
her discomfort with Catholicism in other small&nbsp;
ways, such as requesting removal of specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:22.000 --> 01:52:29.640
Catholic rituals such as the raising of the host&nbsp;
during mass. Elizabeth ensured that her sister’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:29.640 --> 01:52:37.080
funeral was both magnificent and in keeping with&nbsp;
Catholic tradition, although she did imprison&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:37.080 --> 01:52:43.720
a eulogizing priest following it for vaguely&nbsp;
referring to her supposed illegitimacy. 
 
&nbsp;

01:52:46.320 --> 01:52:54.400
On the day of her coronation, the 15th of January&nbsp;
1559, Elizabeth processed through the city of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:54.400 --> 01:53:03.120
London as the people cheered her joyfully. She was&nbsp;
anointed and crowned Queen of England and Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:03.120 --> 01:53:10.240
in Westminster Abbey, entering the church&nbsp;
dressed in magnificent royal robes of gold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:10.240 --> 01:53:19.280
trimmed with ermine and jewels, her long red&nbsp;
hair loose and flowing. As Elizabeth passed by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:19.280 --> 01:53:25.840
attendees promptly cut off pieces of the blue&nbsp;
carpet upon which she walked as souvenirs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.560 --> 01:53:32.320
Ever conscious of how controversial her Protestant&nbsp;
faith still was to many of her subjects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.840 --> 01:53:40.880
Elizabeth’s coronation was a largely Catholic&nbsp;
service. Even within a largely Catholic ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:40.880 --> 01:53:47.720
Elizabeth did not prostrate herself for&nbsp;
the anointing. Instead she simply knelt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:47.720 --> 01:53:55.800
in a dignified fashion. Moreover, as soon as the&nbsp;
Latin mass began, the Queen promptly withdrew to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:55.800 --> 01:54:02.000
a private chamber to change her costume&nbsp;
for the upcoming feast and celebration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:02.640 --> 01:54:09.760
This was clearly designed as a public statement&nbsp;
of her rejection of some of the Catholic rites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:09.760 --> 01:54:16.560
carefully choreographed to indicate to the&nbsp;
political nation that further religious changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:16.560 --> 01:54:24.560
would come in the months ahead. Emerging in&nbsp;
an exquisite costume of purple and ermine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:24.560 --> 01:54:30.320
she headed the procession out of the church,&nbsp;
smiling and graciously greeting members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:30.320 --> 01:54:36.680
cheering crowds. Elizabeth had cleverly&nbsp;
managed to have herself crowned in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:36.680 --> 01:54:42.280
which seemed to placate both Catholics&nbsp;
and Protestants to varying extents. 
&nbsp;

01:54:44.880 --> 01:54:52.120
One of Elizabeth’s first acts as Queen was what&nbsp;
is known as the Elizabethan Religious Settlement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:52.720 --> 01:54:58.760
At her first official parliament, she had&nbsp;
her government pass an Act of Uniformity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:58.760 --> 01:55:04.040
which reinstated the 1549 Book of Common Prayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:04.040 --> 01:55:09.240
that had been a bedrock of her half-brother’s&nbsp;
Protestant settlement, although&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:09.240 --> 01:55:16.440
a few alterations were introduced into it in line&nbsp;
with Elizabeth’s much more moderate Protestantism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:17.480 --> 01:55:23.760
One sticking point concerned the real presence&nbsp;
of Christ’s body and blood in the Eucharist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:24.280 --> 01:55:31.240
known as transubstantiation, which Catholics&nbsp;
believed in and Protestants did not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:32.040 --> 01:55:37.360
Some historians argue that the liturgical&nbsp;
alterations of the Elizabethan era made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:37.360 --> 01:55:45.200
it possible for both Protestants and Catholics to&nbsp;
interpret communion as they pleased. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:45.200 --> 01:55:50.160
because Elizabeth was fond of the ceremony&nbsp;
and pageantry of the Roman Catholic Church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:50.680 --> 01:55:56.480
the traditional Catholic vestments worn&nbsp;
by priests and clerics were preserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:56.480 --> 01:56:04.600
under the new Elizabethan Religious Settlement,&nbsp;
as were the images and crucifixes in churches&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:04.600 --> 01:56:12.240
and monasteries across England. This would become&nbsp;
a major point of contention for more hardline&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.240 --> 01:56:20.920
Protestant reformers in England, who believed&nbsp;
ostentation of this kind was idolatrous. Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:20.920 --> 01:56:26.520
also filled as many bishoprics and archbishoprics&nbsp;
as possible with Protestant clerics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:27.040 --> 01:56:34.640
removing Catholic bishops appointed by Mary in&nbsp;
the process. All in all, Elizabeth imposed a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:34.640 --> 01:56:42.720
moderate form of Lutheran Protestantism in the&nbsp;
late 1550s and early 1560s, but it would never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:42.720 --> 01:56:50.320
please a large cohort of radical Calvinists&nbsp;
in England who became known as the Puritans&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:50.320 --> 01:56:58.120
and who wanted a form of Protestantism based&nbsp;
largely on the theocracy Jean Calvin had created&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:58.120 --> 01:57:06.800
in the city of Geneva in the Swiss Confederacy&nbsp;
back in the 1530s and 1540s. Clashes between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:06.800 --> 01:57:13.920
the government and the Puritans would remain&nbsp;
a constant feature of Elizabeth’s long reign.
&nbsp;

01:57:16.400 --> 01:57:22.280
This disagreement illustrates the level&nbsp;
of religious discord which was prevalent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:22.280 --> 01:57:26.840
not just in England, but across&nbsp;
Europe during the sixteenth century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:27.480 --> 01:57:34.040
Elizabeth’s religious settlement decreed&nbsp;
punishment for refusal to attend Sunday services&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:34.040 --> 01:57:41.880
or for other examples of Catholic failure to&nbsp;
conform, but the penalties were small fines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:41.880 --> 01:57:48.240
which frequently went unenforced, depending&nbsp;
on religious sympathies in a given region.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:48.920 --> 01:57:55.360
Many Catholic clergy and the laity found&nbsp;
it convenient to conform outwardly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:55.360 --> 01:58:03.280
while inwardly retaining their own beliefs.&nbsp;
Indeed, as Elizabeth once wisely declared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:03.280 --> 01:58:10.880
quote: “There is only one Christ, one&nbsp;
faith, all else is a dispute over trifles.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.600 --> 01:58:17.840
The English certainly had their religious&nbsp;
struggles, but under Elizabeth these struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:17.840 --> 01:58:24.760
were considerably less violent than they proved&nbsp;
to be elsewhere in Europe, such as in France&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:24.760 --> 01:58:32.400
where the St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre of&nbsp;
1572 would see Parisian Catholics massacre&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:32.400 --> 01:58:40.480
over 3,000 Protestant Huguenots in a single&nbsp;
day. This was in the midst of forty years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:40.480 --> 01:58:48.680
religious wars in France between the 1560s and the&nbsp;
1590s. Compared to this level of religious strife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:49.280 --> 01:58:56.320
England remained calm under Elizabeth, and more&nbsp;
than a decade would pass after her accession to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:56.320 --> 01:59:04.080
the throne before the religious question erupted&nbsp;
into any form of major violence in England. 
&nbsp;

01:59:06.440 --> 01:59:13.880
England’s administrative, economic and military&nbsp;
infrastructure all needed urgent attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:14.480 --> 01:59:20.360
Henry VIII had spent ruinously during&nbsp;
his reign and neither Edward nor Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:20.360 --> 01:59:27.400
had ruled for long enough to fully correct the&nbsp;
problems he bequeathed to them. Conversely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:27.400 --> 01:59:34.880
wars during both Elizabeth’s half-sibling’s reigns&nbsp;
and harvest failures had compounded the problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:35.440 --> 01:59:41.800
English trade had declined as well, following&nbsp;
the decreasing value of the English currency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:42.440 --> 01:59:49.760
Elizabeth worked closely with her newly&nbsp;
appointed Secretary of State, William Cecil, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:49.760 --> 01:59:58.320
de-facto head of her government for the next forty&nbsp;
years, and Thomas Gresham, financier, merchant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:58.320 --> 02:00:05.680
and longtime economic advisor to the Tudors,&nbsp;
to formulate a strategy to rescue the economy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:06.280 --> 02:00:15.440
By 1561 all of the debased currency had been&nbsp;
withdrawn from circulation, dissolved and replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:15.440 --> 02:00:22.680
with freshly minted coins of gold and silver&nbsp;
that re-established confidence in the face value&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:22.680 --> 02:00:30.360
of the English coinage. The recovery of England’s&nbsp;
currency helped the economy rebound nicely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:30.920 --> 02:00:40.000
Prices stabilized and trade boomed once more. In&nbsp;
addition, Gresham founded London’s Royal Exchange&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:40.000 --> 02:00:47.760
early in the reign, after being inspired by his&nbsp;
direct experiences of the Antwerp Bourse where&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:47.760 --> 02:00:54.600
English cloth merchants had been carrying out much&nbsp;
of their trade in the Low Countries for decades. 
&nbsp;

02:00:57.200 --> 02:01:05.920
An island nation requires ships, for both economic&nbsp;
and military purposes. Elizabeth understood that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:05.920 --> 02:01:13.080
a good deal of investment was needed in this&nbsp;
area, yet, she was still constrained by budgets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:13.840 --> 02:01:20.680
Therefore the process of naval expansion was&nbsp;
a careful and practical one during her reign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:21.440 --> 02:01:26.840
The ships built during Elizabeth’s reign&nbsp;
tended to be multi-purpose in design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:27.360 --> 02:01:34.240
meant for trade, exploration, fishing&nbsp;
and warfare. They were smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.800 --> 02:01:41.880
faster and more maneuverable than the standard&nbsp;
warship, and yet not so diminutive that they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:41.880 --> 02:01:48.400
not be equipped with multiple gun carriages.&nbsp;
Several dozen were built for the royal fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:48.400 --> 02:01:55.440
and they were so in a semi-private manner whereby&nbsp;
nobles and mariners offset some of the cost by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:55.440 --> 02:02:02.880
having semi-control over the ships when they were&nbsp;
built. Ships, of course, require experienced and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:02.880 --> 02:02:09.240
competent men to man them and Elizabeth and Cecil&nbsp;
patronised a number of highly skilled mariners&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:09.240 --> 02:02:16.600
from the English West Country, men like Humphrey&nbsp;
Gilbert, Martin Frobisher and Walter Raleigh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:17.120 --> 02:02:25.080
as well as sailors from ports like Bristol who&nbsp;
had decades of experience leading expeditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:25.080 --> 02:02:30.760
to the valuable fisheries off the “Grand&nbsp;
Banks” of Newfoundland in North America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:31.440 --> 02:02:39.400
Their exploration of the North American seaboard&nbsp;
was an important, though often under-appreciated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:39.400 --> 02:02:45.120
precursor to the English colonisation of&nbsp;
North America in the seventeenth century. 
 
&nbsp;

02:02:47.600 --> 02:02:53.680
England’s economic recovery was impressive,&nbsp;
and much credit is due to Elizabeth and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:53.680 --> 02:03:00.840
advisors for overseeing it, especially&nbsp;
since regular taxation was not a strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:00.840 --> 02:03:07.640
England could resort to, as taxes had to&nbsp;
be approved by parliament. Instead a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:07.640 --> 02:03:14.440
source of funding for England’s recovery came&nbsp;
from crown land revenues and customs duties.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:15.160 --> 02:03:20.560
Due to this, Elizabeth’s court was markedly&nbsp;
less extravagant than her father’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:21.280 --> 02:03:26.600
She kept her staff and that of the Privy&nbsp;
Council, the main governing body of the realm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:27.160 --> 02:03:33.280
small, installing most of the members of her&nbsp;
traditional household in positions at court.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:34.000 --> 02:03:40.600
Entertainments and banquets in the early years&nbsp;
were elegant, but not overly extravagant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:41.360 --> 02:03:46.600
Indeed, such was the manner in which Elizabeth&nbsp;
was determined to put the state’s finances&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:46.600 --> 02:03:51.880
on a better footing than had been seen since&nbsp;
the days of her grandfather, King Henry VII,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:52.560 --> 02:04:00.760
that she became notorious for her parsimony and&nbsp;
unwillingness to spend excessive amounts of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:00.760 --> 02:04:07.440
on grand projects. In the long run this&nbsp;
was a judicious policy for the most part&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:07.440 --> 02:04:13.280
and despite its limited resources the English&nbsp;
government accomplished quite a lot during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:13.280 --> 02:04:21.520
Elizabethan era. However, it frequently infuriated&nbsp;
her councillors, who viewed the Queen as a ruler&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:21.520 --> 02:04:29.160
who constantly changed her mind on policy matters,&nbsp;
often based on her concerns about expenditure. 
&nbsp;

02:04:31.720 --> 02:04:37.560
There was one particular area where&nbsp;
Elizabeth could not avoid spending money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:38.200 --> 02:04:47.720
This was in Ireland. When she came to the throne&nbsp;
in 1558, Elizabeth also became Queen of Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:47.720 --> 02:04:55.280
yet the island remained only partially under&nbsp;
English control. Dozens of largely independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:55.280 --> 02:05:02.520
Irish lords ruled different parts of the&nbsp;
country, whereas English rule was largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:02.520 --> 02:05:10.120
confined to Dublin and the Pale which surrounded&nbsp;
it in Leinster. A programme of regional conquest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:10.120 --> 02:05:16.680
and colonisation had been entered into towards&nbsp;
the end of her father’s reign and would play out&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:16.680 --> 02:05:23.720
for decades during Elizabeth’s reign. Initially,&nbsp;
the weaknesses of Elizabeth’s government were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:23.720 --> 02:05:31.400
plain for all to see, as Shane O’Neill, the head&nbsp;
of the foremost Irish lordship in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:31.400 --> 02:05:38.040
the O’Neills of Ulster, raided the Pale&nbsp;
on numerous occasions in the early 1560s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:38.760 --> 02:05:46.920
He was only finally defeated in 1567 because he&nbsp;
overextended himself in the north of the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:46.920 --> 02:05:52.680
and was killed by his erstwhile allies,&nbsp;
the MacDonnells of western Scotland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:53.280 --> 02:06:01.000
Further wars and plantations occurred consistently&nbsp;
through the 1570s and 1580s, gradually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:01.000 --> 02:06:10.200
reducing the Irish lords, though at a large cost&nbsp;
to Elizabeth’s exchequer, much to her dismay. 
&nbsp;

02:06:12.720 --> 02:06:19.600
Because Elizabeth was young, unmarried and&nbsp;
childless, the question of her marriage was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:19.600 --> 02:06:29.800
intensely debated all throughout the 1560s and&nbsp;
1570s. England needed an heir. Unlike Edward and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:29.800 --> 02:06:36.800
Mary before her, there was no sibling waiting to&nbsp;
step into the breach and succeed her if Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:36.800 --> 02:06:43.560
died. Furthermore, the closest remaining&nbsp;
heir to the English throne was Mary Stuart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:44.080 --> 02:06:52.720
Queen of Scots, a granddaughter of Margaret&nbsp;
Tudor, Henry VIII’s sister. Mary was a Catholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:52.720 --> 02:06:58.200
and so Elizabeth and her advisors understood&nbsp;
that the preservation of the new Protestant&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:58.200 --> 02:07:05.520
religious settlement in England depended on Mary&nbsp;
Queen of Scots never securing the English throne,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:06.040 --> 02:07:13.640
even though she had the best claim in law to it&nbsp;
if Elizabeth died childless. Therefore, as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:13.640 --> 02:07:19.920
as the reign commenced, the issue of Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
marriage arose and suitors began to appear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:20.640 --> 02:07:26.560
However, much to the great consternation of&nbsp;
her advisors and the political realm at large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:27.080 --> 02:07:32.640
Elizabeth seemed to only appear to be&nbsp;
interested in Robert Dudley, who she&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:32.640 --> 02:07:39.400
appointed as Master of the Horse and made the&nbsp;
Earl of Leicester several years into the reign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:40.040 --> 02:07:48.320
Tongues wagged throughout the late 1550s and&nbsp;
early 1560s about Dudley’s excessive influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:48.320 --> 02:07:55.880
and the nature of his relationship with the&nbsp;
queen. This was compounded in September 1560&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:55.880 --> 02:08:03.560
when Robert’s wife, Amy Robsart, died&nbsp;
suddenly under suspicious circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:04.440 --> 02:08:10.800
Amy was found at the bottom of a staircase&nbsp;
with her neck broken, following which theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:10.800 --> 02:08:17.320
of foul play abounded, with many presuming&nbsp;
Dudley had thrown his wife down the stairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:17.880 --> 02:08:25.000
breaking her neck in the process, in order to&nbsp;
free himself up to marry the Queen. The theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:25.000 --> 02:08:32.840
is generally discounted by most historians today&nbsp;
and Amy’s death was most likely an accident. 
 
&nbsp;

02:08:35.400 --> 02:08:40.800
The reality is that it was always unlikely&nbsp;
that Elizabeth would marry Dudley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:41.360 --> 02:08:47.840
He was an English lord and by the second half&nbsp;
of the sixteenth century it was an accepted norm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:47.840 --> 02:08:53.520
that a monarch like Elizabeth would marry&nbsp;
a member of another European royal family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:54.040 --> 02:09:01.280
the better to cement an alliance of some&nbsp;
kind. Yet Elizabeth did none of these things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:01.960 --> 02:09:09.400
Already in the 1560s she was cultivating the&nbsp;
public image of herself as The Virgin Queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:09.400 --> 02:09:16.720
pledged in marriage to the realm, rather than&nbsp;
a husband, and mother to all of her people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:17.400 --> 02:09:25.160
This campaign manifested itself in Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
speeches, in the portraiture she commissioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:25.160 --> 02:09:31.800
and it even appeared in the poems and plays of&nbsp;
the time. Elizabeth would continue to entertain&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:31.800 --> 02:09:42.160
suitors for her hand, most notably King Eric XIV&nbsp;
of Sweden and Francis, Duke of Alencon and Anjou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:42.160 --> 02:09:48.160
brother of King Henry III of France,&nbsp;
but she ultimately refused them all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:48.840 --> 02:09:55.600
She would remain unmarried her entire life.&nbsp;
It is hard to escape the fact that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:55.600 --> 02:10:02.840
a complicated psychology at work here, Elizabeth&nbsp;
having grown up at the Henrician court where her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:02.840 --> 02:10:10.560
father married and then dispensed with one wife&nbsp;
after another, Elizabeth’s own mother included.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:11.160 --> 02:10:19.440
She eventually determined not to subjugate herself&nbsp;
to a husband in this way, though her court became&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:19.440 --> 02:10:26.680
a peculiar place where a succession of favourites&nbsp;
including Dudley, Sir Christopher Hatton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:26.680 --> 02:10:33.600
Sir Walter Raleigh and later the second Earl&nbsp;
of Essex gained her favour by courting her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:33.600 --> 02:10:41.280
as one would a prospective bride, all the while&nbsp;
knowing that no marriage would ever follow. 
&nbsp;

02:10:43.840 --> 02:10:51.120
In 1562, Elizabeth, who was in her late&nbsp;
twenties by then, contracted smallpox.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:51.800 --> 02:10:57.360
She recovered without significant&nbsp;
long-term damage to her overall health, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:57.360 --> 02:11:04.400
the disease left her face badly scarred, a common&nbsp;
occurrence amongst those who contracted smallpox&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:04.400 --> 02:11:11.760
in early modern times. In order to disguise&nbsp;
the scars she began to wear elaborate makeup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:12.440 --> 02:11:21.200
Unfortunately, the white make-up she wore to mask&nbsp;
her scarred complexion every day was lead-based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:21.200 --> 02:11:28.200
and the vermillion she used to redden her cheeks&nbsp;
and lips contained significant amounts of mercury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:28.880 --> 02:11:36.440
The regular use of these compounds damaged her&nbsp;
skin and possibly contributed to her hair loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:36.440 --> 02:11:42.960
in later years. As this compounded the&nbsp;
problem caused by the smallpox scars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:42.960 --> 02:11:50.120
Elizabeth caked herself in more layers of makeup&nbsp;
and took to wearing elaborate wigs in later life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:50.880 --> 02:11:56.280
Finally, her teeth began to blacken&nbsp;
and decay due to her sweet tooth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:56.960 --> 02:12:04.160
Sugar was a novelty in Europe, one&nbsp;
introduced from the Americas. All of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:04.160 --> 02:12:12.560
would leave her in later life a woman with ravaged&nbsp;
skin, extensive hair loss and rotted teeth. 
 
&nbsp;

02:12:14.960 --> 02:12:20.840
Amongst the achievements of the Elizabethan&nbsp;
period, one which deserves attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:20.840 --> 02:12:28.880
is that this was an early golden age of English&nbsp;
literature. Some of the greatest writers, poets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:28.880 --> 02:12:35.760
and dramatists in English history worked at&nbsp;
this time and were patronised by the queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:35.760 --> 02:12:42.680
and her courtiers. These included the playwrights,&nbsp;
Christopher Marlowe, William Shakespeare&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:42.680 --> 02:12:52.200
and Ben Jonson, and the poets Edmund Spenser and&nbsp;
Philip Sidney. Walter Raleigh too was a poet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:52.200 --> 02:12:58.680
though he is usually more well-known for&nbsp;
his explorations in the Americas. The sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:58.680 --> 02:13:06.200
breadth of Raleigh’s experiences encapsulate the&nbsp;
excitements and pursuits of the Elizabethan age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:06.880 --> 02:13:14.480
He explored North America, founded the first,&nbsp;
albeit short-lived, English colonial settlement&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.480 --> 02:13:22.080
in North America, at Roanoke Island, captured&nbsp;
Spanish gold as a privateer, and is sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:22.080 --> 02:13:29.040
fantastically credited with introducing&nbsp;
tobacco to England and the potato to Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:29.680 --> 02:13:37.320
The exciting new frontiers of exploration and&nbsp;
trade broadened English horizons immensely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:37.320 --> 02:13:43.400
just as the literary flowering did&nbsp;
for English culture. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:43.400 --> 02:13:52.000
between 1576 and 1578, Martin Frobisher&nbsp;
sailed in search of a Northwest Passage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.000 --> 02:14:01.200
while Sir Francis Drake became only the second&nbsp;
person to ever circumnavigate the globe in 1581. 
&nbsp;

02:14:03.680 --> 02:14:10.040
The first permanent colonies were not finally&nbsp;
settled by England in North America until the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:10.040 --> 02:14:17.000
early seventeenth century, but much of the&nbsp;
groundwork in terms of exploration and mapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:17.000 --> 02:14:23.800
was completed during Elizabeth’s reign. A&nbsp;
major drive in all of this was a desire to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:23.800 --> 02:14:31.680
strike in an indirect fashion at Spain, the&nbsp;
pre-eminent colonial power in the Americas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:31.680 --> 02:14:38.520
by attacking Spanish shipping in the Atlantic&nbsp;
and trying to present a threat in North America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:39.120 --> 02:14:46.200
The first years of Elizabeth’s reign had seen&nbsp;
fairly good relations between England and Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:46.200 --> 02:14:52.240
King Philip II of Spain having only recently been&nbsp;
King of England through his marriage to Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:52.240 --> 02:15:01.520
Mary. But relations gradually soured in the course&nbsp;
of the 1560s as Elizabeth refused Spanish offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:01.520 --> 02:15:08.160
for her to marry Philip, while her religious&nbsp;
settlement also put England at loggerheads with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:08.160 --> 02:15:15.680
Catholic Spain. Finally, there were a number of&nbsp;
diplomatic clashes over shipping in the Atlantic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:16.320 --> 02:15:23.840
By the 1570s, England and Spain were&nbsp;
involved in a sort of unofficial cold war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:23.840 --> 02:15:30.480
but it would take until the second half&nbsp;
of the 1580s before open war commenced. 
&nbsp;

02:15:33.040 --> 02:15:40.680
All of this was tied up with an ongoing succession&nbsp;
crisis, in which the religion of Elizabeth’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:40.680 --> 02:15:48.760
likely heir, Mary Queen of Scots, was the&nbsp;
main issue. Elizabeth refused to acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:48.760 --> 02:15:57.440
the Catholic Mary as her potential successor&nbsp;
in the 1560s and 1570s. The Queen of Scots&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:57.440 --> 02:16:04.040
was a Catholic, and for Elizabeth to encourage&nbsp;
her claim would be to open herself up to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:04.040 --> 02:16:10.720
plots and determination of Catholics to place&nbsp;
one of their own on the throne of England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:10.720 --> 02:16:18.920
and so restore Catholicism, Elizabeth hoped&nbsp;
to contain Mary’s ambitions through diplomacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:19.440 --> 02:16:26.120
but this proved to be impossible. Mary had lived&nbsp;
out her youth in France with her mother’s family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:26.640 --> 02:16:33.240
but she returned to Scotland in 1561 to&nbsp;
take up her rightful position as queen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:33.880 --> 02:16:40.560
Mary’s presence in Scotland would prove&nbsp;
inflammatory. Her Catholicism aroused the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:40.560 --> 02:16:47.840
animosity of the Scottish Protestant Lords of&nbsp;
the Congregation, who in 1560 had established&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:47.840 --> 02:16:56.280
a form of Calvinist Protestantism in Scotland as&nbsp;
the state religion. Mary was soon a major source&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:56.280 --> 02:17:03.240
of contention in Scotland and for years to come&nbsp;
in the 1560s conspiracies surrounded her.  
 
&nbsp;

02:17:05.720 --> 02:17:12.481
As with Elizabeth to the south in England, there&nbsp;
was a major debate surrounding Mary’s potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:12.480 --> 02:17:20.080
marriage. At one time rumours had abounded that&nbsp;
Dudley would head north to Scotland and marry&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:20.080 --> 02:17:31.640
Mary, but instead she wed Henry Stuart, Lord&nbsp;
Darnley, in 1565. Darnley and Mary were cousins&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:31.640 --> 02:17:36.441
and were both grandchildren of&nbsp;
Margaret Tudor, Henry VIII’s sister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:37.080 --> 02:17:45.720
They were also both Catholics. As such, this&nbsp;
marriage posed an enormous threat to Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:45.720 --> 02:17:52.000
and the Protestant settlement in England,&nbsp;
particularly so once Mary have birth to a son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:52.520 --> 02:18:01.000
the future King James VI of Scotland, in the&nbsp;
summer of 1566. It was assumed that he would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:01.000 --> 02:18:07.640
raised as a Catholic in keeping with his mother’s&nbsp;
religion. There then followed an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:07.640 --> 02:18:15.000
series of events in which Mary began an affair&nbsp;
with James Hepburn, the earl of Bothwell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:15.560 --> 02:18:24.040
who was then accused of murdering Darnley in&nbsp;
the spring of 1567. Mary married Bothwell within&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:24.040 --> 02:18:31.800
weeks of Darnley’s suspicious death and then was&nbsp;
imprisoned not long after by the Scottish lords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:31.800 --> 02:18:39.120
who were appalled at her conduct. She was then&nbsp;
forced to abdicate in favour of her infant son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:39.640 --> 02:18:46.441
James, who succeeded as King James VI&nbsp;
of Scotland at just one year of age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:47.200 --> 02:18:54.000
He would now be raised by some of the seniormost&nbsp;
Scottish lords in the Protestant faith. 
 
&nbsp;

02:18:56.480 --> 02:19:01.839
These developments might by themselves have&nbsp;
not been such a bad thing for Elizabeth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:01.840 --> 02:19:08.160
the English government had matters&nbsp;
remained there. But within months&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:08.160 --> 02:19:14.080
Mary had escaped from her imprisonment at&nbsp;
Lochleven Castle and, after an abortive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.080 --> 02:19:21.240
attempt at reclaiming the Scottish throne,&nbsp;
absconded over the border into northern England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:21.960 --> 02:19:29.481
This placed Elizabeth and her ministers in London&nbsp;
in a very difficult position. Mary could not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:29.480 --> 02:19:37.559
sent back to Scotland. To do so would potentially&nbsp;
lead to her execution by the Scottish lords and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:37.560 --> 02:19:44.720
Elizabeth was unwilling to send a fellow queen&nbsp;
to that fate. At the same time, her presence as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:44.720 --> 02:19:52.080
a Catholic contender to the English throne on&nbsp;
English soil made her a focus of conspiracies&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:52.080 --> 02:20:00.240
by Roman Catholics in England and abroad. In&nbsp;
Rome, English Catholic exiles openly discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:00.240 --> 02:20:07.280
the possibility of launching a revolt in England&nbsp;
against Elizabeth’s government and then placing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:07.280 --> 02:20:14.880
Mary on the English throne as a means to restore&nbsp;
England yet again to the Catholic fold. 
 
&nbsp;

02:20:17.440 --> 02:20:26.280
This was not idle speculation. In 1569 a major&nbsp;
revolt was initiated by some of the leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:26.280 --> 02:20:33.240
Catholic lords of the north of England. Although&nbsp;
she was not directly involved in plotting this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:33.800 --> 02:20:40.760
Mary’s position was important in the Northern&nbsp;
Rebellion. Though it was quickly crushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:40.760 --> 02:20:48.840
and Elizabeth ordered the execution of some 750&nbsp;
Catholic rebels, it highlighted the precarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:48.840 --> 02:20:57.920
situation with Mary present on English soil,&nbsp;
doubly so when Pope Pius V issued a Papal Bull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.440 --> 02:21:05.400
Regnans in Excelsis in 1570 in response to&nbsp;
the suppression of the Northern Rebellion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:05.920 --> 02:21:13.320
through which Elizabeth was excommunicated and&nbsp;
all true Catholics were enjoined to overthrow her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:14.040 --> 02:21:20.920
This volatile situation would prevail for&nbsp;
nearly twenty years. Though Elizabeth never&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:20.920 --> 02:21:28.720
met Mary in person, the deposed Scottish queen&nbsp;
would spend the entirety of the late 1560s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:29.240 --> 02:21:38.360
the 1570s and much of the 1580s under house arrest&nbsp;
in a series of different palatial houses across&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:38.360 --> 02:21:45.719
northern England. Many of Elizabeth’s senior&nbsp;
ministers such as Cecil would doubtlessly have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:45.720 --> 02:21:53.720
been delighted to have an opportunity to simply&nbsp;
have Mary killed and the threat she posed removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:53.720 --> 02:21:59.241
but Elizabeth simply refused to do&nbsp;
this to a fellow female monarch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:59.840 --> 02:22:07.200
Again, there was a complex psychology to this and&nbsp;
we should remember that Elizabeth’s mother Anne&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:07.200 --> 02:22:14.160
was a queen who had been executed three decades&nbsp;
before Mary arrived to northern England. 
 
&nbsp;

02:22:16.480 --> 02:22:22.919
In the complex situation which developed with Mary&nbsp;
Queen of Scots over the next decade and a half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:22.920 --> 02:22:30.680
Sir Francis Walsingham, the English Secretary&nbsp;
of State and chief spymaster from 1573 onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:31.200 --> 02:22:38.920
was paramount. A committed Protestant,&nbsp;
Walsingham viewed himself as a religious warrior&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:38.920 --> 02:22:45.441
whose work was to defend the Protestant settlement&nbsp;
in England from the threat Mary and others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:45.960 --> 02:22:51.600
such as radical members of the English&nbsp;
Catholic exile community in Rome, posed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:52.200 --> 02:22:58.240
He had personally witnessed the mass slaughter of&nbsp;
Protestants at the St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:58.240 --> 02:23:05.000
in Paris while he was serving as England’s&nbsp;
ambassador there and he was determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:05.000 --> 02:23:11.600
not to see the same thing happen in his&nbsp;
own country. Many plots by Catholics in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:11.600 --> 02:23:19.800
England and abroad were rooted out and crushed by&nbsp;
Walsingham and his spies in the 1570s and 1580s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:20.560 --> 02:23:28.640
Then, finally, in 1586 he was presented with an&nbsp;
opportunity to end the greatest threat of all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:29.320 --> 02:23:37.400
That year Walsingham became aware of a plot&nbsp;
centred on Anthony Babington, an English Catholic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:37.400 --> 02:23:43.559
to assassinate Elizabeth and free Mary&nbsp;
before placing her on the throne of England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:44.240 --> 02:23:52.120
Walsingham knew what was going on from very early&nbsp;
on in affairs, but rather than reveal it quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:52.120 --> 02:23:59.800
he placed double agents within Babington’s circle&nbsp;
and allowed him not only to incriminate himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:59.800 --> 02:24:08.280
but Mary as well. When he finally revealed the&nbsp;
Babington Plot, not even Elizabeth could ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:08.280 --> 02:24:16.240
Mary’s treachery. She was placed on trial, found&nbsp;
guilty of treason and executed at Fotheringay&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:16.240 --> 02:24:24.440
Castle on the 8th of February 1587. Elizabeth&nbsp;
had finally been coerced into signing Mary’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:24.440 --> 02:24:33.160
death warrant after nearly two decades of keeping&nbsp;
her imprisoned in northern England. Yet she had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:33.160 --> 02:24:41.160
nearly faltered at the last, attempting to rescind&nbsp;
the order after she had given it. In the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:41.160 --> 02:24:47.559
it was only because Walsingham, Cecil and other&nbsp;
members of the Privy Council had acted speedily&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:47.560 --> 02:24:54.960
after she signed Mary’s death warrant that the&nbsp;
order was carried out, the councillors being aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:54.960 --> 02:25:02.520
that Elizabeth would probably change her mind on&nbsp;
reflection. Killing a fellow queen was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:02.520 --> 02:25:09.600
hardest decisions Elizabeth ever made, regardless&nbsp;
of how much of a threat Mary had posed. 
 
&nbsp;

02:25:12.160 --> 02:25:18.480
The drama surrounding Mary’s presence in&nbsp;
England for so many years was connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:18.480 --> 02:25:26.679
to the wider international situation in the&nbsp;
1570s and 1580s. When it came to foreign policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:27.200 --> 02:25:32.200
Elizabeth had always sought to&nbsp;
avoid an expensive outright war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:32.880 --> 02:25:38.360
Her discreet encouragement of privateering and&nbsp;
the frequent attacks on Spanish bullion ships by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:38.360 --> 02:25:47.000
figures like Francis Drake were a cost-effective&nbsp;
way of waging an unofficial cold war with Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:47.000 --> 02:25:54.000
as was providing financial and material support&nbsp;
to the Dutch rebels who were engaged in a war of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:54.000 --> 02:26:02.160
independence against Spain in the Netherlands&nbsp;
from 1568 onwards. She would doubtlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:02.160 --> 02:26:08.080
have continued in this vein had it not been for&nbsp;
events in France where the Wars of Religion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:08.600 --> 02:26:15.960
a series of civil wars between Catholics,&nbsp;
Protestants and a politique faction that steered&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:15.960 --> 02:26:25.720
a middle way between the two, had been underway&nbsp;
since the 1560s. In the 1580s, Philip II of Spain&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:25.720 --> 02:26:32.160
was beginning to intervene there on the side&nbsp;
of the French Catholic League, while his armies&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:32.160 --> 02:26:39.240
had also made advances in the Netherlands against&nbsp;
the Dutch. Faced with the possibility of the Dutch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:39.240 --> 02:26:45.920
revolt being crushed and France falling into&nbsp;
the Spanish camp, Elizabeth and her councillors&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:45.920 --> 02:26:54.560
determined that they had no other option but to&nbsp;
risk an outright war with Spain. In August 1585&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:55.120 --> 02:27:01.519
they agreed the Treaty of Nonsuch with the Dutch&nbsp;
and Elizabeth’s old love, Robert Dudley, the Earl&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:01.520 --> 02:27:08.281
of Leicester, headed for the continent with an&nbsp;
English expeditionary force to aid the Dutch&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:08.280 --> 02:27:16.360
in the Netherlands. The First Anglo-Spanish War&nbsp;
had commenced after years of shadow fighting. 
&nbsp;

02:27:19.120 --> 02:27:26.200
Elizabeth expected Leicester to aid the Dutch&nbsp;
and to keep their rebellion alive, but without&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:26.200 --> 02:27:32.960
committing too many English resources. However,&nbsp;
he immediately scuppered this by accepting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:32.960 --> 02:27:38.720
position of Governor-General of the Netherlands&nbsp;
from the Dutch States General, something which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:38.720 --> 02:27:44.920
committed Elizabeth to defending the Dutch against&nbsp;
Spanish and Catholic aggression indefinitely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:45.640 --> 02:27:52.000
She penned a sharp rebuke and ordered that it&nbsp;
be read in Dudley’s presence before the States&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:52.000 --> 02:27:58.800
General, but the damage was done by then.&nbsp;
With the signing of the Treaty of Nonsuch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:27:59.320 --> 02:28:04.200
Leicester’s subsequent actions in the Low&nbsp;
Countries and the execution of Mary Queen of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:04.200 --> 02:28:13.160
Scots early in 1587, the Spanish began preparing&nbsp;
an armada to try and invade and conquer England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:13.160 --> 02:28:20.120
the ultimate goal being to depose Elizabeth&nbsp;
and return the country to the Catholic fold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:20.800 --> 02:28:27.800
In anticipation of this, Elizabeth authorized&nbsp;
Drake to lead an expedition to the Spanish port of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:27.800 --> 02:28:34.920
Cadiz and attack the Spanish fleet that was being&nbsp;
prepared there before it could ever leave Spain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:35.600 --> 02:28:44.321
The raid was a great success, with dozens of ships&nbsp;
destroyed, but it only stalled Philip II’s plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:44.320 --> 02:28:53.080
for a time. By the spring of 1588 the lost ships&nbsp;
had been replaced and on the 12th of July 1588,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:28:53.080 --> 02:29:01.400
a Spanish armada of approximately 130 ships set&nbsp;
sail for the English Channel. The plan was for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:01.400 --> 02:29:07.440
armada to sail through the Channel, pick up the&nbsp;
Spanish armies in the Netherlands and then convey&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:07.440 --> 02:29:15.120
an army of tens of thousands of Spanish troops&nbsp;
to southern England. Against this Spanish Armada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:15.640 --> 02:29:21.321
the English had hundreds of ships, but&nbsp;
most of these were small merchant ships&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:21.320 --> 02:29:28.080
and the vessels of privateers, not the kind of&nbsp;
warships that comprised the Spanish fleet. 
&nbsp;

02:29:30.680 --> 02:29:36.800
The odds seemed to be against an English victory&nbsp;
and on land an army was being prepared for if&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:36.800 --> 02:29:42.680
the Spanish successfully made landfall.&nbsp;
The goal, of course, was to try and ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:42.680 --> 02:29:48.840
prevent the armada from making contact with&nbsp;
the Spanish land forces in the Netherlands or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:48.840 --> 02:29:56.000
landing in England thereafter. The conclusive sea&nbsp;
battle took place over approximately two weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:29:56.760 --> 02:30:02.920
On the 19th of July 1588, the Spanish&nbsp;
fleet was first sighted off Plymouth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:03.640 --> 02:30:11.361
The next day, the English sailed out to fire on&nbsp;
the Spanish. The English ships proved much faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:11.360 --> 02:30:17.839
and more maneuverable, but few were willing to&nbsp;
get close enough to inflict any significant damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:17.840 --> 02:30:25.720
and so only one Spanish ship was taken in these&nbsp;
initial clashes. In the days that followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:25.720 --> 02:30:31.361
the Spanish continued to advance eastwards&nbsp;
through the Channel, eventually anchoring off&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:31.360 --> 02:30:40.200
the north coast of France. This was the sign for&nbsp;
the English to move. On the 29th of July, Raleigh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:40.760 --> 02:30:47.361
Drake and others oversaw the launching of numerous&nbsp;
fireships at the Spanish Armada in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:47.360 --> 02:30:55.080
the night. These were small ships set on fire&nbsp;
and effectively used as primitive rockets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:30:55.800 --> 02:31:01.600
The attack was enough to force the Spanish away&nbsp;
from the French coast without having rendezvoued&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:01.600 --> 02:31:08.640
with the main land army. The weather took over&nbsp;
from there as an unseasonable storm suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:08.640 --> 02:31:14.600
ripped through the Channel and scattered the&nbsp;
Spanish fleet northwards into the North Sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:15.280 --> 02:31:21.440
The English pursued the fleeing Spanish ships&nbsp;
north, but in the ensuing severe weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:21.440 --> 02:31:26.480
many Spanish ships were wrecked off&nbsp;
the coasts of Scotland and Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:26.480 --> 02:31:32.360
with less than a third of the original&nbsp;
Armada, eventually limping home to Spain. 
&nbsp;

02:31:34.920 --> 02:31:42.960
On land, news of this miraculous storm only&nbsp;
arrived gradually. Therefore, on the 8th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:42.960 --> 02:31:50.600
August, Elizabeth rode out to review her troops&nbsp;
at Tilbury, who were mustering to defend England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:51.120 --> 02:31:56.280
under the command of the Earl of&nbsp;
Leicester. There, dressed in white&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:31:56.280 --> 02:32:03.200
and wearing a magnificent silver breastplate,&nbsp;
she gave the most famous speech of her reign:
&nbsp;

02:32:03.840 --> 02:32:12.560
“I am come…to live and die amongst you all –&nbsp;
to lay down for my God, and for my kingdoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:12.560 --> 02:32:21.401
and for my people, my honour and my blood,&nbsp;
even in the dust. I know I have the body of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:21.400 --> 02:32:30.240
a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and&nbsp;
stomach of a king – and of a King of England too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:30.880 --> 02:32:39.880
and think foul scorn that…any prince of Europe&nbsp;
should dare to invade the borders of my realm…I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:40.800 --> 02:32:46.600
myself will take up arms – I&nbsp;
myself will be your general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:32:46.600 --> 02:32:53.000
judge, and rewarder of every one&nbsp;
of your virtues in the field.”
 
&nbsp;

02:32:53.680 --> 02:32:59.800
The troops cheered in response to this&nbsp;
heartening speech from the Queen and made ready,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:00.320 --> 02:33:06.320
little knowing that they were already safe and&nbsp;
that the Spanish Armada had been destroyed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:06.320 --> 02:33:13.320
the winds of the North Sea. There would be other&nbsp;
armadas sent by both Spain and England against one&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:13.320 --> 02:33:22.080
another in a war which would last until 1604,&nbsp;
but the armada of 1588 has become infamous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:22.680 --> 02:33:30.880
as it could well have resulted in the conquest&nbsp;
of England by Spain had it not been for the storm&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:30.880 --> 02:33:37.080
which destroyed it and the calculated actions&nbsp;
of Elizabeth’s sea captains in the Channel. 
 
&nbsp;

02:33:39.640 --> 02:33:45.600
Some historians have referred to&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s remaining years following 1588,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:45.600 --> 02:33:53.520
as the “second reign.” England’s economy began&nbsp;
to suffer due to the costs of war with Spain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:33:53.520 --> 02:34:00.201
taxes mounted and prices rose as the country&nbsp;
experienced a series of poor harvests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:00.960 --> 02:34:09.520
Additionally, by the 1590s, most of Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
old guard of advisors, courtiers, and friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:09.520 --> 02:34:16.920
were dead and gone, their positions taken over&nbsp;
by a younger generation of ambitious courtiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:17.640 --> 02:34:25.840
Leicester died just weeks after the defeat of the&nbsp;
Armada in 1588. Walsingham passed two years later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:26.600 --> 02:34:34.281
Cecil survived down to 1598, though he had&nbsp;
begun to pass his responsibilities to his son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:34.840 --> 02:34:43.840
Robert, years before that. By then the queen was&nbsp;
an aging woman, surrounded by courtiers who played&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:43.840 --> 02:34:51.520
up to her ideas of courtly love, but secretly&nbsp;
rolled their eyes at her aged ways in private,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:51.520 --> 02:34:58.281
criticising her seeming unwillingness to allow&nbsp;
the party of war hawks led by Robert Devereux,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:34:58.280 --> 02:35:06.960
second Earl of Essex, to take the war to Spain&nbsp;
more aggresively. Even in old age, Elizabeth was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:06.960 --> 02:35:14.000
unwilling to finance such a costly approach.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, a factional conflict developed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:14.000 --> 02:35:21.400
at the English court between Essex and Robert&nbsp;
Cecil, each side acquiring their own allies. 
&nbsp;

02:35:24.040 --> 02:35:32.680
There was one area where costs spiralled utterly&nbsp;
in the 1590s. In 1593 the lords of Ulster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:33.200 --> 02:35:40.160
led by Hugh O’Neill, second earl of Tyrone,&nbsp;
and Hugh O’Donnell, lord of Tyrconnell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:40.680 --> 02:35:47.441
launched a new massive rebellion against&nbsp;
the English crown. They felt they had little&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:47.440 --> 02:35:53.120
option except to do this in the face of an&nbsp;
ever-expanding English state in Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:35:53.800 --> 02:36:00.321
The Nine Years’ War would pose the greatest&nbsp;
existential threat to English rule yet seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:00.320 --> 02:36:08.599
in Ireland, as O’Neill had armed his men with&nbsp;
guns and trained them in modern military tactics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:09.360 --> 02:36:15.960
The Irish also sought aid from Spain and a Spanish&nbsp;
expedition would even arrive to Kinsale in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:15.960 --> 02:36:24.360
south of the country in 1601. By then the Irish&nbsp;
were in charge of the vast majority of the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:24.360 --> 02:36:30.040
having inflicted the biggest defeat against&nbsp;
an English army there under the Tudors at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:30.040 --> 02:36:38.080
the Battle of the Yellow Ford on the 14th of&nbsp;
August 1598. In the end, Elizabeth would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:38.080 --> 02:36:46.440
to finance the provision of an enormous army for&nbsp;
Ireland in the late 1590s and early 1600s, over&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:46.440 --> 02:36:54.360
15,000 men at its height, in order to avoid the&nbsp;
English being expelled from the island altogether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:36:55.080 --> 02:37:01.840
Her cost-saving measures here over the years&nbsp;
had ultimately cost her money in the long run. 
&nbsp;

02:37:04.360 --> 02:37:12.519
The final blow emotionally for Elizabeth was a&nbsp;
betrayal by Essex, her newest court favourite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:12.520 --> 02:37:18.800
a stepson of Leicester’s and in some ways&nbsp;
his successor in her affections. In his&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:18.800 --> 02:37:26.720
early twenties, handsome and brash, the young Earl&nbsp;
felt no shame in taking liberties with the Queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:26.720 --> 02:37:33.640
who was both flattered and indulgent with&nbsp;
him. She gifted him monopolies on wine imports&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:33.640 --> 02:37:40.120
and continually appointed him to military&nbsp;
positions. Elizabeth sent Essex to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:40.120 --> 02:37:48.120
Ireland to crush the rebellion there in 1599,&nbsp;
supported by an army of an unprecedented size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:48.760 --> 02:37:57.200
She was enraged when he met secretly with&nbsp;
the leader of the Irish rebels, Hugh O’Neill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:37:57.200 --> 02:38:05.320
just a few months after arriving in Ireland and&nbsp;
then left again for England without leave to do so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:05.320 --> 02:38:11.559
and having accomplished nothing there. When he&nbsp;
burst into Elizabeth’s private chambers upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:11.560 --> 02:38:20.161
his return to London, the queen determined that&nbsp;
these offences could not stand. She stripped him&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:20.160 --> 02:38:26.559
of his offices and priveleges and ostracised him&nbsp;
from court when she learned that Essex had spoken&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:26.560 --> 02:38:33.920
publicly afterwards of seeing her without her&nbsp;
wig and her layers of poisonous make-up. Essex&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:33.920 --> 02:38:42.560
was placed under house arrest and deprived of his&nbsp;
monopolies. In desperation, in February 1601, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:42.560 --> 02:38:50.121
sought to lead an insurrection against Elizabeth,&nbsp;
not to depose her, but to extract concessions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:50.120 --> 02:38:58.200
her and force the removal of his great rival,&nbsp;
Robert Cecil. However, he was unable to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:38:58.200 --> 02:39:06.720
enough support to be successful, and was executed&nbsp;
for treason on the 25th of February 1601.
&nbsp;

02:39:09.160 --> 02:39:14.200
Essex’s death seemed to drain the&nbsp;
last of Elizabeth’s energies from her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:14.960 --> 02:39:21.440
By then, the Queen was growing older and weaker&nbsp;
all the time, and it fell to Robert Cecil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:21.960 --> 02:39:27.800
now Elizabeth’s chief minister and the undisputed&nbsp;
head of the government after he had orchestrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:27.800 --> 02:39:34.800
Essex’s destruction, to oversee a subtle&nbsp;
resolution of the succession question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:35.480 --> 02:39:42.639
He knew that if Elizabeth had thus far refused to&nbsp;
name a successor, she was not going to do so now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:43.360 --> 02:39:51.320
Hence, Cecil entered into secret negotiations with&nbsp;
King James VI of Scotland, the son of Mary Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:51.320 --> 02:39:59.200
of Scots. James was now well into his thirties and&nbsp;
a successful king in his own right in Scotland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:59.760 --> 02:40:06.960
He was also a firm Protestant and he remained&nbsp;
the nearest claimant to the English throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:07.680 --> 02:40:12.880
From England, Cecil advised him of the&nbsp;
manner by which he should write to Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:13.440 --> 02:40:21.519
to put her in the best frame of mind to name him&nbsp;
as her heir. James listened well and his overtures&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:21.520 --> 02:40:28.640
were received warmly by the Queen. Elizabeth&nbsp;
would never formally name him as her heir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:29.200 --> 02:40:34.559
but she was more than aware in her final&nbsp;
years that unless she named somebody else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:35.120 --> 02:40:40.360
the throne would inevitably fall to&nbsp;
her Scottish cousin once she died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:41.080 --> 02:40:49.000
Thus, in her own strange way, Elizabeth had made&nbsp;
preparations for the end of the House of Tudor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:49.000 --> 02:40:56.400
the rise of the House of Stuart to unite England&nbsp;
and Scotland under one king, something which&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:40:56.400 --> 02:41:03.440
successive English monarchs of the medieval era&nbsp;
had failed to achieve through force of arms. 
 
&nbsp;

02:41:06.040 --> 02:41:14.000
In March 1603, Elizabeth fell seriously ill.&nbsp;
She was nearing her seventieth year by then.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:14.800 --> 02:41:19.280
Suffering from a fever and from&nbsp;
sores in her mouth and throat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:19.280 --> 02:41:27.599
Cecil urged her to take to her sickbed, saying she&nbsp;
must rest. “Little man,” she snapped back at him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:28.160 --> 02:41:36.080
“must is not a word to use to princes,” showing&nbsp;
that even at the end she was still a force to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:36.080 --> 02:41:42.480
reckoned with. She died in her bed at Richmond&nbsp;
Palace not long after this, on the morning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:42.480 --> 02:41:52.040
the 24th of March 1603. She was 69 years old and&nbsp;
had ruled England for forty-four and a half years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:41:52.800 --> 02:42:00.000
Besides her few mementoes of Leicester and her&nbsp;
other loved ones, a locket ring was found among&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:00.000 --> 02:42:08.400
the Queen’s few obviously sentimental possessions.&nbsp;
The ring opened to reveal two tiny miniatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:08.960 --> 02:42:16.120
one of Elizabeth and one of another young&nbsp;
woman dressed in a much older Tudor fashion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:16.840 --> 02:42:23.680
Historians theorize that the woman featured in&nbsp;
the second miniature was her mother, Anne Boleyn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:24.400 --> 02:42:30.839
Elizabeth had never known her, Anne having been&nbsp;
executed on her father’s orders when Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:30.840 --> 02:42:38.720
was just two years old. But her untimely&nbsp;
death had hung like a shadow over Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:39.280 --> 02:42:46.920
dictating many of her choices, notably&nbsp;
her unwillingness over almost two decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:42:46.920 --> 02:42:53.481
to order the execution of Mary Queen of&nbsp;
Scots, and her own decision to never marry. 
&nbsp;

02:42:56.000 --> 02:43:03.080
In the weeks following Elizabeth’s death, James VI&nbsp;
and his Scottish entourage made their way south at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:03.080 --> 02:43:11.040
a leisurely pace, meeting his new subjects along&nbsp;
the way. Cecil had put everything in place and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:11.040 --> 02:43:19.960
James was crowned in Westminster Abbey on the 25th&nbsp;
of July 1603 as James I of England and Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:19.960 --> 02:43:26.840
and VI of Scotland. In Ireland, the injection&nbsp;
of thousands of troops and the appointment&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:26.840 --> 02:43:33.840
of an effective military commander to oversee&nbsp;
affairs there, Charles Blount, Lord Mountjoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:34.400 --> 02:43:42.440
had seen O’Neill and his Irish confederates&nbsp;
defeated in the spring of 1603. Tyrone surrendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:42.440 --> 02:43:50.240
a week after Elizabeth’s death, believing she was&nbsp;
still alive, though James ultimately offered very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:50.240 --> 02:43:58.599
lenient peace terms. With the war over in Ireland,&nbsp;
and both Elizabeth and her Spanish nemesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:43:59.160 --> 02:44:08.639
Philip II, dead, the pathway to peace opened up&nbsp;
and the Treaty of London in 1604 brought the First&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:08.640 --> 02:44:16.840
Anglo-Spanish War to an end. It was something of&nbsp;
a stalemate, but given that England had survived&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:16.840 --> 02:44:24.960
war with the superpower of the sixteenth century,&nbsp;
it was very much an English victory. The reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:24.960 --> 02:44:31.000
that followed would see many of the projects which&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s reign had commenced come to fruition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:31.560 --> 02:44:38.321
with the first English colonies settled in&nbsp;
North America at Jamestown in Virginia in 1607&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:38.320 --> 02:44:46.360
and in New England from 1620 onwards. England&nbsp;
would remain a Protestant state therafter. 
 
&nbsp;

02:44:48.920 --> 02:44:56.520
Elizabeth I was a very complicated&nbsp;
woman. During a long reign of 45 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:44:57.120 --> 02:45:01.680
she presided over one of the most&nbsp;
important periods in English history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:02.400 --> 02:45:09.759
When she rose to the throne England was in a&nbsp;
confused state, toing and froing over and back&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:09.760 --> 02:45:17.600
between Roman Catholicism and Protestantism, while&nbsp;
as a state within Europe England had declined&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:17.600 --> 02:45:23.600
enormously from being one of the most centralised&nbsp;
and powerful kingdoms of medieval Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:23.600 --> 02:45:31.241
to becoming something of a minnow in the fifteenth&nbsp;
century, wracked by instability and civil war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:32.000 --> 02:45:37.680
All of that changed during Elizabeth’s&nbsp;
reign. The duration of it allowed for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:37.680 --> 02:45:44.640
firm establishment of the country as a moderate&nbsp;
Protestant power, while her long confronatation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:44.640 --> 02:45:50.520
with Spain reasserted England as a force&nbsp;
to be reckoned with in European politics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:45:51.200 --> 02:46:00.400
The small kingdom effectively defeated Spain, the&nbsp;
superpower of the day, in the Anglo-Spanish War&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:00.400 --> 02:46:07.200
and provided crucial support to the Dutch rebels&nbsp;
in the early days of their war of independence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:08.000 --> 02:46:14.200
Ireland was about to be reduced fully to English&nbsp;
rule and while the first permanent English&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:14.200 --> 02:46:19.679
colonies were not settled in the Caribbean and&nbsp;
North America until the reign of her successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:19.680 --> 02:46:26.520
King James I, the groundwork was&nbsp;
firmly laid during Elizabeth’s reign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:27.240 --> 02:46:35.599
And yet there were strange elements to her time as&nbsp;
queen. She was a woman who was haunted by the fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:35.600 --> 02:46:42.640
her mother had suffered at the hands of her father&nbsp;
and who spent much of her life prevaricating over&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:42.640 --> 02:46:49.401
both the prospect of marrying and removing&nbsp;
the threat posed by Mary Queen of Scots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:50.120 --> 02:46:57.480
In the end, there is no doubt that her reign&nbsp;
was a success, though its success was owing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:46:57.480 --> 02:47:05.200
much to figures like Drake, Raleigh, Cecil and&nbsp;
Walsingham as it was to queen herself.  
 
&nbsp;

02:47:08.120 --> 02:47:14.400
What do you think of Queen Elizabeth I?&nbsp;
Was she truly England’s greatest queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:15.000 --> 02:47:21.600
or have historians simply lionized her because&nbsp;
of successes which were achieved during her reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:21.600 --> 02:47:26.361
which had little to do with her&nbsp;
own role as ruler of the realm?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:47:27.080 --> 02:47:34.559
Please let us know in the comment section, and in&nbsp;
the meantime, thank you very much for watching!

